1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Ball-of-Hunting-Creek/6000000005388662337
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Richard Ball''' was born ABT 1639 in London, Middlesex, England, and died BEF 11 JUL 1677 in Patapsco, Baltimore Co., MD. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >In a late number of the "Virginia Magazine," Dr. Christopher Johnston, of Baltimore, writes of a "Missing Ball," one Richard, who married in Maryland and died there, leaving one child, a daughter, to whom he devised property."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of William Ball  and Hannah Atherold.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mary Kinsey 1 Mar 1661/62 in Baltimore City Co., MD, daughter of Hugh Kinsey and Margaret Coulton. She was born ABT 1645 in Anne Arundel Co., MD, and died BEF FEB 1677 in Patapsco, Baltimore Co., MD.  She was Thomas Hum
2 CONC phrey's widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Richard BALL and Mary KINSEY is:
2 CONT # Hannah BALL was born ABT 1663 in Baltimore City Co., MD, and died 16 APR 1702.  Married Thomas Everest.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Supporting data
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I032786&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT >Most of these names occur soon after in the Quaker records of the province, so that is seems quite probable that they were a little group of Quaker colonists who had come up from Virginia to take up the recently opened lands on t
2 CONC he Patapsco in Baltimore County, which appears to have been erected into a county this same year. [1659] The Gorsuches, Powells, Claphams, Dickensons, '''Balls, Kinseys (Kenseys) and Humphreys''' are known to have come from Lancas
2 CONC ter County ... 
2 CONT
2 CONT >p. 246 ...Charles Gorsuch, August 3, 1661, had surveyed for him "Whetstone Point," 50 acres comprising that part of the present city of Baltimore now occupied in part by Fort McHenry... William Ball and Richard Ball, each of whom
2 CONC  received warrants for 500 acres of land under the warrant of 1659 and became settlers on the Patapsco, have been shown by Dr. Christopher Johnston to be the sons of Colonel William Ball of Lancaster County, the progenitor of the 
2 CONC distinguished Virginia family of that name (Virginia Mag. Vol. VII p. 440 and Vol. VIII p. 80)*. Hugh Kinsey and Thomas Humphrey have been shown by Mr. Miles White, in his Ancestry of Johns Hopkins, to have also come from Lancaste
2 CONC r County (Publications Southern Historical Association, Vols. IV, p. 395 and V, p. 360)...
2 CONT ...many if not all of these first actual settlers [those named in the Warrant of 1659] were probably a group of friends who came up together from Lancaster County and that the probable motive of their migration was religious perse
2 CONC cution, as most of them are known to have been Quakers, and it was at this time that Virginia began to put into full effect the various repressive measures against the numerous recent converts to this sect in order to drive them o
2 CONC ut of that colony.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hughes 2006-09-26 16:24:06 
2 CONT Supplement to Early Settlers Query
2 CONT
2 CONT Ball, Richard, Mr.  AA:342 Film No.:  Of Anne Arundel County, transported himself by 1661, married Mary, relict & executrix of Thomas Humphreys, about 1671, & died by 1677, leaving a daughter, Hannah Ball  Transcript. 5:20; 6:48; 
2 CONC 7:117; 20:10  Original. CC:106; WC:509  MSA SC 4341-
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hughes 2006-09-26 16:34:08
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage References
2 CONT
2 CONT *Richard, m. by 1 March 1661, Mary, wid. of Thomas Humphreys (ARMD 67:134; BALR RM#HS:19). 
2 CONT * William, m. by 1677, Mary, widow of Thomas Humphreys (ARMD 67:134). [this record seems to mean William [sic: Richard] was married to Mary by 1677, when she is noted in the Archives of Maryland]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=thamm&id=I479
2 CONT
2 CONT >!Commisioned a justice of Baltimore Co 4 Jul 1665, May 19 1672, June 5 1674 and March 2 1675/6 (Md. Arch, III, 529; XV, 38, 68, 71; Lib, C D, 64)
2 CONT
2 CONT >23 Feb 1677/78: Patent to Hannah Ball daughter of Richard Ball, late of Baltimore Co., Deceased, for 300 acres in Baltimore Co. called East Humphreys. The patent recites that the said tract was patented 13 Feb 1659, to Thomas Hum
2 CONC phreys, who died leaving issue, one daughter, Mary and devised the land by will to his wife, Mary Humphreys. The said Mary Humphreys, widow of Thomas, conveyed the land by deed of gift to her daughter, Mary Humphreys, and shortly 
2 CONC after married Richard Ball, by whom she had one daughter, Hannah Ball. Mary Humphreys, daughter of Thomas and Mary, obtained a resurvey on the land and a patent on 10 Jun 1671 but subsequently died without issue. Richard Ball and 
2 CONC Mary his wife are both dead and William Ball admistrator of Richard Ball petitions for an extension of the patent to Hannah Ball sole daughter of Richard and Mary as heir to her sister Mary Humphreys. (Land Office, Lib 20, Fol 10)
2 CONC . William G. Stanard, editor, The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol, VIII, No. 1, Jun 1901, p. 83. Google Books, online 
2 CONT
2 CONT >!11 Feb 1683: Thomas Everest, of the Clifts, Calvert county, and Hannah, his wife, daughter and heir of Richard Ball, late of Baltimore county, deceased, convey to John Bennett, of Anne Arundel county, merchant, a tract of 60 acr
2 CONC es in Baltimore county, '''called Ball's Addition, taken up and patented by the said Richard Ball gent'''., dec'd father of the said Hannah Everest. (Balt. Co., I R. M., no. H. S., 72.) William G. Stanard, editor, The Virginia Mag
2 CONC azine of History and Biography, Vol, VIII, No. 1, Jun 1901, p. 82. Google Books, online
2 CONT
2 CONT >!1688: Died before his father and not mentioned in his father's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Also see
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * See: "A Forgotten Member of the Ball Family" (Anonymous), The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol. 7, No. 4, p. 440-441 (April, 1900) and Vol. 8, No. 80-8x (July, 1900).
2 CONT * Re: Richard Ball b. abt 1645 Norfolk Co., VA m. Elizabeth Linton b. Bef. 1651 Norfolk Co, VA. Excerpt from Ball Family Records By Rev. William Ball Wright, M.A. 1908 ÃÂ "Before coming to Virginia, Colonel William Ball married H
2 CONC annah Atherall of ÃÂ Burgh, Suffolk, England, by whom he had four children, Richard, William, Joseph, and Hannah. "Richard, the eldest son of Colonel William Ball, died in infancy," says Rev. Horace Edwin Hayden, in his work "Vir
2 CONC ginia Genealogies." There has been, however, for generations a tradition in the Ball family of Lancaster County that this son was of a peculiar disposition, preferring a life of solitude and isolation. The tradition says further t
2 CONC hat his father gave him an estate in Richmond County, then a part of Lancaster, and that he lived there a bachelor, and died there. The place is now the property of a gentleman residing in Essex County, Virginia, and is called "Co
2 CONC bham Park." A few years ago, while having some cleaning done, a brick grave was discovered, over which there was a marble slab similar to those covering the graves of the early Balls at White Chapel Church. Time and neglect have a
2 CONC lmost obliterated the inscription, but enough is left to show that it is the grave of a Ball, and the old heads of the family then living, recollecting the tradition, pronounced it the grave of Richard Ball. In a late number of th
2 CONC e "Virginia Magazine," Dr. Christopher Johnston, of Baltimore, writes of a "Missing Ball," one Richard, who married in Maryland and died there, leaving one child, a daughter, to whom he devised property."ÃÂÃÂÃÂÃÂ Who are Ri
2 CONC chard Ball and Elizabeth Linton? 1998
2 CONT
