2 CONT Father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances, Anne and Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was the father of Mary Ball, wife of Augustine Washington, and mother of President George Washington. Therefore, Joseph is George Washington's maternal grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Married Well and Often, Marriages of the Northern Neck of VA, 1649 - 1800, Headley
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=113366639
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Ball (Richard , Colonel William , William, John II, John Paris, William, Robert) was born 1670 in Stafford County , Virginia, and died abt. 1722 in Stafford County, Virginia. He married Winnifred Williams abt.1694 in Stafford
2 CONC  County, Virginia. She was born 1676 in Charles town, Charles County, Maryland.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''This Joseph (b. 1649)  couldn't be the son of Richard (b. 1640) and Elizabeth Ball (b. 1646), but I don't know who he belongs to? Any help welcome!'''
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Joseph Ball (1649-1711) was born in England and came to Virginia sometime before his father's death, making his home at a plantation called Epping Forest. He served as a justice of the county court, a vestryman for his church pari
2 CONC sh, as a Burgess (in 1698, 1700, and 1702), and as a lieutenant colonel in the county militia. Ball was married twice. From his first marriage to Elizabeth Rogers (or Romney), who died by the early 1700's, he had five children: An
2 CONC ne Ball (later the wife of Colonel Edwin Conway), Elizabeth Ball (later the wife of the Reverend Joseph Carnegie),Esther or Easter Ball (later the wife of Rawleigh Chinn), Hannah Ball (later the wife of Rawleigh Travers, II, and a
2 CONC fterwards the wife of Simon Pearson), and Joseph Ball (died 1760).
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his first wife, Joseph Ball married Mary Johnson (died 1721), a widow who had two children from her previous marriage. Together they had one child, a daughter named Mary Ball (1708-1789). Joseph passed away, how
2 CONC ever, soon after when Mary Ball was only three years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Johnson Ball was remarried the following year to a man named Richard Hewes, who left her a widow for the third time in 1713. Her daughter, Mary Ball, was completely orphaned by the age of 12 or 13 and was subsequently raised 
2 CONC by a guardian named George Eskridge, a local lawyer, land speculator, and Burgess, whose plantation was called Sandy Point. In 1731, at the age of 23, she married Augustine Washington (1694-1743), a middle-aged widower with three 
2 CONC children, who ranged in age from 13 to 9. Their first child, a little boy they named George Washington (1732-1799), was born early the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography: Broun, Thomas L.  "The Ball, Conway, Gaskins, McAdam and other kindred of William and Janetta Broun of Northern Neck, VA," William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine 20, 60-8.
2 CONT
2 CONT Freeman, Douglas Southall. George Washington: A Biography, Volume I. New York: Scribner's, 1948. See particularly, 42-47 and 530-534.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heck, Earl Leon Werley. Colonel William Ball of Virginia, the great-grandfather of Washington. London: S.M. Dutton, 1928.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jones, Christine Adams. Queenstown: Early Port Town of Lancaster County, Virginia, 1692. Lancaster, Virginia: Mary Ball Washington Museum & Library, 1980.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierce, Elizabeth Combs. "Mary Johnson, second wife of Col. Joseph Ball," William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine 15, no. 2: 176-7.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
