1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Joseph Ball==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Born Oct 15, 1680 VA  
2 CONT * Died Sep 11, 1721 Northumberland Co. VA 
2 CONT * Parents: William Ball of Millenbeck, Margaret Williamson
2 CONT * Married:  Mary Mottram
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Ball became guardian of Sydney Mottrom, son of Spencer Mottrom, 1671-1698?, and younger brother of the wife, Mary, of Joseph's son, Spencer Ball.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's uncle, Joseph Ball, 1649-1711, son of William Ball, had a daughter Mary with his second wife. This daughter, Mary Ball, 1708-1789, was President George Washington's mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://genforum.genealogy.com/spencer/messages/6437.html
2 CONT
2 CONT >The Joseph Ball who married Mary Spencer was the older Joseph's nephew. The young Joseph's grandfather, Col. Wm. Ball and Mary Spencer's father, Col. Nicholas Spencer were quite good friends. For a long time I confused the two Jo
2 CONC seph's until last summer when I visited Northumberland Co., Virginia. The records show that between the Washington's and the descendants of Joseph Ball & Mary Spencer there was a second cousin relationship. Hope this helps
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources:==
2 CONT
2 CONT # Church, Randolph W., Asst. Reference Librarian, Library of the University of Virginia, undated Report of the contents of the Fox Family Bible, on library letterhead, typed circa 1933, Fox Family Collection no. 3262, box 1888.
2 CONT # "Mottrom-Wright-Spencer-Ariss-Buckner," Wm. & Mary Coll. Quarterly Hist. Mag., ser. 1, 17:53-59, 1908.
2 CONT # Hayden, Horace E., "Ball Family", Virginia Genealogies...., Wilkes-Barre PA, 1891, p.65-66.
2 CONT # McKenzie, Colonial Families of the United States, 2:43-44.
2 CONT # William & Mary Coll. Quart. Hist. Mag., 1s21:103, 1912-13.
2 CONT # [http://books.google.com/books?id=yeWgvfDpwbwC&lpg=PA795&ots=4YNZOML5oZ&dq=Joseph%20ball%20mary%20Spencer&pg=PA795#v=onepage&q=Joseph%20ball%20mary%20Spencer&f=false Lewis of Warner Hall:] The History of a Family.  Page 795.  "B
2 CONC all Family."
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://feindholloway.com/ball/jb1680.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Ball b. 1680 m. Mary Mottrom
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida J. Lee, _Abstracts of Lancaster County, VA, Wills 1653-1800_ (Baltimore, 1973), p. 7: "Ball, Sarah. Will. 6 Aug. 1742. Rec. 10 Sept. 1742. Legatees: Daus. Easter Glascock, Margaret Ball, John Selden. Gr. son Wm. Montague, Gr. 
2 CONC daughter. Frances Lawry. Extr. Wm. Glasscock. Mention is made of a marriage bet. her gr. son Richard Selden and the daughter. of James Ball. Wits. Margaret Williamson, Eliz. McTire, and Wm. Ball. W.B. 13, p. 301." "Inventory and A
2 CONC ppraisement. Rec. 9 October 1742. Returned by Wm. Glasscock, Extr. W.B. 13, p. 290. Division of Negroes held by Sarah Ball as dower of estate of her husband Richard Ball, dec'd. recorded 12 Nov. 1742. "Divided between Mrs. Margare
2 CONC t Ball, Mr. Wm. Glasscock, Mr. Richard Selden and Mr. William Montague. W.B. 13, p. 294."
2 CONT
2 CONT _The Registers of North Farnham Parish 1663-1814 and Lunenburg Parish 1783-1800, Richmond County, VA_, compiled and published by George Harrison Sanford King (Fredericksburg, VA, 1966), p. 87: "M. Harding, William and Sarah Ball, 
2 CONC 14 February 1730/1. [MRC, p. 87 questioned the surname of the bride - if Bale or Ball; the latter is correct. She was the only daughter of Captain Joseph Ball (c. 1680-1721) as Hayden, p. 65, states, but he errs in stating her mot
2 CONC her was Mary Spencer. An Act of the House of Burgesses in May 1732 regarding land in Essex County (British Colonial Office 5/1389, ff. 70-71) proves that Captain Joseph Ball married Mary Mottrom, the only child of Spencer Mottrom,
2 CONC  Gent. (1670-1700) of Northumberland County, and he was the only son and heir of John Mottrom and his wife nee Hannah Fox whose father David Fox of Lancaster County made her a deed poll on 11 November 1662 for 800 acres on Piscata
2 CONC way Creek which fell into Essex County. This land descended to the aforementioned Mary Mottrom and this Act states she married the aforementioned Captain Joseph Ball and William Loury, Gent. of Essex County and had five children b
2 CONC y each spouse all of whom were living in 1732."
2 CONT
2 CONT Wm & Mary Quarterly, series 1, Vol. 17, no. 4 (Apr. 1909), p. 238: "Register of St. Stephen's Parish, Northumberland County. (Extracts).
2 CONT * "Spencer Ball, son to Joseph, was born March 14, 1707.
2 CONT * "Richard Ball, son to Joseph, was born Oct. 25, 1710.
2 CONT * "Joseph Ball, daughter [sic] to Joseph, was born March 8, 1712.
2 CONT * "Sarah Ball, daughter to Joseph, was born March 10, 1714. . . .
2 CONT * "Spencer Mottrom Ball, son to Spencer Ball, was born Sept. 9, 1736."
2 CONT
2 CONT This sequence of names makes it clear that the Joseph Ball who married Mary Mottrom is the father of Spencer, Richard, Joseph, and Sarah and the grandfather of Spencer Mottrom Ball.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright 1999 Kathleen Much Copying is permitted for noncommercial, educational use by individual scholars and libraries. This message must appear on all copied material. Any other use, including electronic reproduction or distri
2 CONC bution, requires written permission of the author.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Capt. Joseph Ball, 1720 In the name of God Amen. I, Joseph Ball, of the County of Northumberland, being sick and weak of body, but of perfect mind and memory, praise be to Almighty god for the same, and calling to mind the
2 CONC  uncertainty of this life, that it is but a blast, I do therefore make and ordain this to be by last Will and Testament in manner and form following.
2 CONT
2 CONT Imprimis, I give my soul to God that gave it to me in full and certain hope of pardon and remission for all my sins, and through the merits of my dear and blessed Saviour Jesus Christ, and my body to be decently buried according t
2 CONC o the discretion of my Executors hereinafter named: and as for what temporal estate it has pleased Almighty God of his great goodness to bestow upon me, I give and bequeath as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give to my two sons, Spencer and Richard Ball, all that tract of land that I now live on, being about 1500 acres, which was formerly settled on them by deed, but supposing that Spencer's part will take some of the land abov
2 CONC e the glade, my desire is that the glade shall be the division between them; and I do give my son, Spencer Ball and his heirs forever, one negro man named Gie, in consideration that he make over to my son, Richard Ball, and his he
2 CONC irs male forever, lawfully begotten of his body, all his right, title and interest of the land aforesaid, lying above the glade, and for the want of such issue, to return again to my son Spencer, &c., and in case my son Spencer fa
2 CONC il to perforn the same as aforesaid, then I give the aforesaid negro to my son, Richard Ball, and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, I give to my daughter, Sarah Ball, my land in Richmond Co., to her and her heirs forever, provided my loving wife give each of my two sons, viz: Joseph and William Ball, part of her land at Black Point, but in case my wife s
2 CONC hould not give my said sons part of her land, that then I give my land in Richmond Co. to my said two sons, Joseph and William Ball, and their heirs forever. I give my dear and loving wife all the negroes I had with her and one ne
2 CONC gro boy named Tom and after her decease I give the said Tom to my son, Richard Ball, one negro man name Cuffy, to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, I give to my three children, viz., Sarah, Joseph and William Ball, six negroes, viz., Harry, Jenny, Nan, Hannah, Winny and Billy, to them and their heirs forever, and my will is that each of my said children shall have their
2 CONC  proportional share of the said negroes and their increase delivered to them as they, my said children, shall come of age, or at the day of marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, I give my son Spencer my young black horse. Item, I give all the rest of my estate not heretofore given, unto my dear wife and five children, viz, Spencer, Richard, Sarah, Joseph and William Ball, to be equally divided among
2 CONC  them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, my will is that my loving brothers, Richard and Geo. Ball, have the tuition of my children and the management of their estate; notwithstanding it is my desire that they do not take my children from their dear mother, upon an
2 CONC y account than as they shall think reasonable for the better educating them nor their estate without she marry again, and that her then husband should prove unkind to my children, and not take provident care of them and their esta
2 CONC te, that then my desire is that my said brothers take upon them the whole care of my children and the management of their estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, I do hereby nominate and appoint my dear and loving wife executrix of this my last Will and testament, and I do revoke and make null and void all former Wills and Testaments by me made, and I do hereby ratify and confirm thi
2 CONC s to be my last Will and Testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT In witness wereof, I hereunto set my hand and seal this the 19th day of 8ber, 1720.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, sealed and delivered in the presence of David Ball, Jno. Jossy, Jno. Lacy. Die 9ber 15, 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probated, North'd Co., "to be the last Will and Testament of Capt. Joseph Ball, dec'd," &c. Teste Richard Lee, Cl'k Ct.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://wc.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=adboyd&id=I5408
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Citation:
2 CONT
2 CONT An Act of the House of Burgesses in May 1732 regarding land in Essex County (British Colonial Office 5/1389 , ff. 70-71)
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT An Act of the House of Burgesses in May 1732 regarding land in Essex County (British Colonial Office 5/1389 , ff. 70-71) proves that Captain Joseph Ball married Mary Mottrom, the only child of Spencer Mottrom, Gent. (1670-1700 ) o
2 CONC f Northumberland County, and he was the only son and heir of John Mottrom and his wife nee Hannah Fox whose father David Fox of Lancaster County made her a deed poll on 11 November 1662 for 800 acres on Piscataway Creek which fell
2 CONC  into Essex County. This land descended to the afore mentioned Mary Mottrom and this Act states she married the afore mentioned Captain Joseph Ball and William Lowry, Gent. of Essex County and had five children by each spouse all 
2 CONC of whom were living in 1732. The Registers of North Farnham Parish 1663-1814 and Lunenburg Parish 1783-1800, Richmond County, VA, compiled and published by George Harrison Sanford King (Fredericksburg, VA , 1966), p. 87:
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
