1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Capt. George Ball (1683-1746) of "Bay View" in Northumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Genealogies of Virginia Families", pg. 474:
2 CONT
2 CONT >Thomas Dameron, and Winifred were witnesses to a will March 20, 1726. Winifred died shortly after this and Thomas Dameron married about 1730, his third wife, Ann, widow of John Pope and daughter of Capt. George Ball. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Capt. George Ball, son of Capt. William Ball, was born about 1680.''' According to family history, he was married twice. The surname of his first wife is unknown. By this first marriage there were two or three children, among 
2 CONC them Ann, born probably in 1702 or 3. The court records clearly prove that George Ball was married to Grace Haynie, daughter of Anthony and Sarah Harris Haynie before 1709, as Anthony Haynie in his will dated June 31, 1709, mentio
2 CONC ns his daughter Grace Ball.
2 CONT
2 CONT >The land inherited by Mrs. Sarah Haynie Waddy from her father, Major John Harris, is the famous "Bayview" plantation, given to her daughter Grace Haynie Ball, which has remained in the possession of some of the descendants of Cap
2 CONC t. George and Grace Haynie Ball to this present time. This land was adjacent to the Dameron plantation and, in 178_, George Dameron brought suit against Major Harris over their boundary lines, and a final settlement was made by th
2 CONC e official survey of Capt. John Haynie.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Estate 
2 CONT * George BALL, in his 1746 Northumberland Will left bequests to his wife, Grace HAYNIE Ball; his mother-in-law Sarah HARRIS (1) Haynie (2) Waddy, named as   Sarah WADDY; and SEVEN children: John BALL married to Margaret; George BA
2 CONC LL; David BALL; Richard BALL; Joseph BALL, Sarah BALL Dameron and Harris BALL, unmarried daughter.  His son George BALL was his Executor; his sons, John, David and Richard BALL were his witnesses.
2 CONT
