1 OCCU Mother of George Washington, Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A121971  DAR  Ancestor #: A121971]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball was the mother of President George Washington. The father of our country.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Ball_Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball Washington was born as Mary Ball in 1708 in Lancaster County, Virginia, British America. She was the only child of Joseph Ball and his second wife, the widow Mary Johnson  [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball met Augustine Washington and they married in 1730. It was her first marriage and his second. Augustine had four children with his first wife, Jane Butler Washington however, only 2 of them lived to adulthood. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Together, Mary Ball and Augustine Washington had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT #George - (1732-1799) 
2 CONT #Betty - (1733-1797) 
2 CONT #Samuel - (1734-1781) 
2 CONT #John Augustine - (1736-1787) 
2 CONT #Charles - (1738-1799) 
2 CONT #Mildred - (1739-1740) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustine died in 1743. Unlike most widows in Virginia at the time, Mary Ball Washington never remarried. She lived to see her son, George Washington, inaugurated as President in 1789. She died four months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball Washington was buried on the Lewis Plantation a few steps from "Meditation Rock." Tradition has it that this was her favorite retreat for reading, prayer, and meditation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many monuments to Mary Ball Washington in Fredericksburg, Virginia, where she lived from 1772 until her death in 1789. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The house purchased for her by her son George has been preserved by APVA Preservation Virginia and is open to the public as a historic house museum. It contains a fine collection of antique furnishings, some with Washington family
2 CONC  provenance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ball Washington is buried near Kenmore, the former home of her daughter and son-in-law Fielding and Betty Lewis. Kenmore is also open regularly for public tours. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A monument to Mary Ball Washington was erected in 1833 and dedicated by President Andrew Jackson. But was left unfinished until a new one was dedicated by President Grover Cleveland in 1894. [2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The University of Mary Washington, a public university in Fredericksburg, Virginia, was also named after Mary Washington. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington's relationship with his mother was forever strained. Although she was by no means poor, she regularly complained to outsiders that she was destitute and neglected by her children, much to George's embarrassment. Animosi
2 CONC ty between mother and son persisted until her death from cancer in the first year of his presidency. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mary Ball Washington Museum and Library is a museum and historical archive in the Northern Neck of The Chesapeake Bay, Virginia. Its purpose is to preserve the history of Lancaster County, Virginia. It opened in 1958 and was n
2 CONC amed in honor of George Washington's mother, Mary Ball, a Lancaster County, Virginia native and granddaughter of the ca 1653 emigrant, William Ball I.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Library has an extensive collection of books and genealogical research material dating back to 1651, covering some 350 years of history in Lancaster County in particular and the Northern Neck in general, as well as Middlesex a
2 CONC nd Essex counties. It contains approximately 10,000 books, periodicals and manuscripts concerning Virginia and the Northern Neck, as well as research material on adjoining states. The Museum seeks to recapture the stories and the 
2 CONC rich history of the people of The Northern Neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT The museum and library are open to the public, who may tour the historic buildings, view exhibits, participate in educational programs and trace family histories.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to the museum and library, the five building complex, located in the Lancaster Courthouse Historic District, includes the Old Jail (1820), Clerk's Office (c. 1797), Lancaster House and the Steuart Blakemore Building (c
2 CONC .1900).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
