2 CONT
2 CONT Encyclopedia:
2 CONT Augustine (Austin) Washington: half brother of George Washington.
2 CONT Lawrence Washington: half brother of George Washington.
2 CONT John Washington, grandfather of George Washington, migrated from Englandto Virginia in 1657. The ancestral home, Sulgrave, Hamptonshire has aWashington Monument. John's wife name is not know. AugustineWashington, George Washington
2 CONC 's father, went to school in England. Hewas a successful Virginia landowner, planter, and owned an ironworks,etc. Augustine married Jane Butler; they had four children; Lawrenceand Augustine (called Austin) were two of them; Lawre
2 CONC nce married Ann(Nancy) Fairfax, daughter of Col. William Fairfax, cousin and agent ofLord Fairfax. Augustine married Mary Ball, daughter of COL. William Balland mother of George. Augustine and Mary had six children; Augustinedied 
2 CONC 12 Apr 1743, and George's half brother, Lawrence reared him. GeorgeWashington was born 22 Feb 1732 [11 Feb, old style ((GregorianCalendar)], in Westmoreland County, Virginia. He married Martha Custis,a widow.
2 CONT The Washington/Haynie Family Connection:Various Interviews: George Ball(born: about 1683) was the grandson of COL. William Ball, and the nephewof Mary Ball, George Washington's mother. George Ball married GraceHaynie, daughter of 
2 CONC Anthony Haynie and Sarah Harris Haynie (Sarah'ssecond husband was Francis Waddy). Anthony was the son of CPT John Haynie(1624-1697) and Jane Morris.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have the following description of Mary Ball Washington directly fromthe pen of Lawrence Washington, half brother of George as follows:
2 CONT "Of the mother, I was ten times more afraid than I ever was of my ownparents. She awed me in the midst of kindness, for she was truly kind.And even now when time has whitened my locks, and I a grandparent of thesecond generation, 
2 CONC I could not behold that Majestic woman withoutfeelings it is impossible to describe. Whoever has seen thatawe-inspiring air and manner, so characteristic in the Father of HisCountry, will remember the matron as she appeared when t
2 CONC he presidinggenius of her well-ordered household, commanding and being obeyed."
2 CONT In seeking for an American woman upon whom eyes of past generations havebeen drawn, and on whom the admiration of coming ages is likely to rest,we have even more than a patriotic honor in choosing Mary the mother ofGeorge Washingt
2 CONC on.
2 CONT Fortunately for the curiosity and instruction of the world, a manconnected with the family but in no wise related to her, George W. P.Custis, a grandson of the widow Custis, who married George......(http://www.altlaw.com/edball/mb
2 CONC c.htm)
1 NOTE from Colonial Families of the Southern States of America;
2 CONT 9-6. MARY, b. 1707 at "Epping Forest," lancaster Co., Va; d. Aug 25, 1789 at "Mt. Vernon, Fairfax Co, Va; m. march 6, 1739 Augustine Washington, of Bridges Creek, Westmoreland Co. Va., b. 1694, d Apr 12, 1743; son of Lawrence and 
2 CONC Mildren (Warner) Washington)  Mother of George Washington
1 NOTE !BIRTH - VIRGINIA LINEAGES, LETTERS & MEMORIES by Alice Jean Nelson, p. 271
2 CONT Mary Ball born 1708 in Lancaster Co., daughter of Joseph Ball and wife, Mary
2 CONT Montague Johnson.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 19
2 CONT Mary Ball was the only child of her parents, Col. Joseph Ball and Mary Montague, the widow Johnson whom Joseph Ball married early in 1708.  Col. Joseph Ball is described as of St. Mary's White Chapel, the county of Lancaster, VA.,
2 CONC  Gentleman.  Epping Forest, the historic homestead is located in Lancaster Co.,VA.  The old mansion, a portion of which is the house in which Mary Ball was born, stands in a grove on the southwest side of the highway.  The estate 
2 CONC of Epping Forest, with the exception of a few years, has been held continuously by members of the Ball family.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT Married Capt. Augustine Washington in 1731.  They were the parents of George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT Died 25 August 1789 at Fredericksburg, VA.
