2 NOTE George Washington, 1st President of the United States is your 9th cousin five times removed.
3 CONT You  → Raymond Henry Ashworth, Sr. (your father) → Susie Gertrude Ashworth (Smith) (his mother) → Mary Reba Robie (Smith) (Walker) (her mother) → Abner Chaffee Walker (her father) → William C. Walker (his father) → Dea. William Wa
3 CONC lker (his father) → Pvt. Ebenezer Walker, II (his father) → Ebenezer Walker (his father) → Jane Walker (Metcalf) (his mother) → Michael Metcalf (immigrant) (her father) → Rev. Leonard Metcalf (his father) → Brian Metcalf, of Bear 
3 CONC Park (his father) → Margaret Metcalfe (Pigot) (his mother) → Isabel Pigot (Gascoigne), Countess of Pigot (her mother) → Sir William “the Younger” Gascoigne, V (her brother) → Elizabeth Tailboys (Gascoigne) (his daughter) → Lady An
3 CONC ne Dymoke (Tailboys) (her daughter) → Frances Windebank (Dymoke) (her daughter) → Mildred Reade (Windebank) (her daughter) → Col. George Reade, Esq., Acting Gov. (her son) → Mildred Warner (Reade) (his daughter) → Mildred Gale (Wa
3 CONC rner) (her daughter) → Augustine Washington, Sr. (her son) → George Washington, 1st President of the United States (his son)
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/64/47/b1/5b/53444864142808f5/800px-gilbert_stuart_williamstown_portrait_of_george_washington_3__original.jpg?hash=5afe2d908c360cf5d813f7c288fce6a624fff0afedb27062adb6a3b517317466.1794297599
2 FORM jpg
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/38/cc/2c/f3/53444863fa98a781/1798_eleanor_parke_custis_portrait_of_geo_original.jpg?hash=7cf84ed5a65fafcfe31ddfd2c9b547987335ecec7f95b8ca4dce497b0833f3a6.1794297599
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Silhouette Portrait of George Washington
2 NOTE Eleanor Custis, known as "Nelly," was the granddaughter of Martha Washington and adopted stepdaughter of George Washington (Nelly Custis’s paternal grandfather was Daniel Parke Custis, Martha Washington’s first husband). Custis gr
3 CONC ew up at Mount Vernon, George and Martha Washington’s estate, then moved with the family in 1789 to the seat of government in New York following the election that made her step-grandfather the first president of the United States.
3 CONC  A year later, the family moved to Philadelphia, where they would remain until Washington’s retirement from office in 1797. The art courses that Custis took while living in New York and Philadelphia likely included silhouette-cutt
3 CONC ing instruction, priming her to render this hollow-cut silhouette portrait of George Washington, in which the negative profile was cut from board and placed atop a black fabric support. Custis created a silhouette portrait of Mart
