2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Washington (1734–1781) [Son of Capt. Augustine Washington, Sr. and Mary (Ball) Washington] was a brother of United States President George Washington. He was born on November 27, 1734 [O.S. November 16, 1734], at Pope's Cre
2 CONC ek, Wakefield, Westmoreland County, Virginia.[1] He died at age 46 on September 26, 1781, a couple of days before the decisive Franco-American victory of Yorktown. His probable cause of death was from tuberculosis, or a similarly 
2 CONC contagious and recurring respiratory disease.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel served in numerous posts in Stafford County, Virginia, including justice of the peace, county magistrate, county sheriff, militia officer, and parish vestryman. He resided at Mount Vernon from 1735 to 1738.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had Harewood, a Georgian-style mansion near then Charles Town, Virginia, designed by the renowned architect John Ariss in 1770. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1973.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages and children===
2 CONT Samuel married five times[4] and had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Jane Champe (1724–1755)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Mildred Thornton (about 1741–1763) – possibly died during or shortly after childbirth. Her cousin, also named Mildred Thornton, married Samuel's younger brother Charles.
2 CONT # Thornton Washington (1760–1787)
2 CONT # Tristram Washington (born 1763) birth date is wrong, Samuel's third wife Lucy died in 1762
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  Lucy Chapman (1743–1762) – died during childbirth
2 CONT # Infant Washington (1762–1762)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Anne Steptoe (1737–1777)
2 CONT # Ferdinand Washington (1767–1788)
2 CONT # George Steptoe Washington (1773–1809) – He married Lucy Payne, sister of Dolley Madison, wife of James Madison. His son Samuel Walter Washington (1799–1831) married Louisa Clemson, sister of Thomas Green Clemson.
2 CONT # Lawrence Augustine Washington (1775–1824)
2 CONT # Harriot Washington (1776–1822)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Susannah Perrin (1740–1783)
2 CONT # John Perrin Washington (1781–1784)
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT # "Col Samuel Walter Washington". GENi genealogy website. Retrieved June 25, 2014.
2 CONT #  "A proud Washington descendant". Bedinger Family History and Genealogy. Retrieved June 25, 2014.
2 CONT # National Park Service (2008-04-15). "National Register Information System". National Register of Historic Places. National Park Service.
2 CONT # Ambler, Charles Henry. "George Washington and the West". Historic Pittsburgh Text Collection. University of Pittsburgh. Retrieved September 20, 2013.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of COLONEL.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A121972  DAR Ancestor # A121972]
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: P379201909 
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Samuel WASHINGTON 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: M 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 16 NOV 1734 in Wakefield, Stafford Co, VA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 9 SEP 1781 in Harewood, Virginia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 131
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Washington was born at Wakefield, on Pope's Creek, Westmoreland Co., VA., the birthplace of his brother George and his sister Elizabeth (Betty) on November 27 (N.S.), 1734, "about 3 in ye Morni ng."
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived as a boy in 3 different counties of Virginia, Westmoreland, Prince William and Stafford; as a man he lived and held public office in three, Stafford, Frederick and Berkeley, but he never wa s an old man; he died at the ag
2 CONC e of 47. Harewood was his home for 11 years, 1770 to 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN ALEXANDER, A NORTHERN NECK PROPRIETOR, HIS FAMILY, FRIENDS & KIN by Wesley Pippenger
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Samuel Washington was born 16 Nov. 1734 at Wakefield in Stafford Co. He went to Jefferson Co., W.V. where he died 1781. His estate "Harewood". He was the son of Augustine Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: There is some differences in the above but found that Samuel Washington was in the county of Stafford at that time. - JMC
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #1 - Ibid, p. 143
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Champe, daughter of Col. John Champe of King George Co.,VA. If she had any children they died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #2 - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT Mildred Thornton, daughter of Col. John Thornton and his wife Mildred Gregory. Mildred had two sons: Thornton, born probably in 1760 who grew up and married; and Tristram, born in or about 1763, wh o did not grow up.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #3 - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Chapman, daughter of Nathaniel Chapman died in 1762 of childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #4 - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Steptoe, daughter of Col. William Steptoe. She is said to have been the widow of Willoughby Allerton when she married Samuel Washington, probably in the autumn of 1766. She had at least 4 Washin gton children:: Ferdinand, bor
2 CONC n July 16, 1767; George Steptoe, Lawrence Augustine and Harriet, who married Parks. All of Anne's children must have been born prior to December 1780, since Samuel Washi ngton married his fifth wife not later than that date.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR #5 - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT The Widow Holding, who seems to have been born Susannah Perrin. She had a son, John Perrin Washington, who was born prior to Sept. 9, 1781 when Samuel Washington made his will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - See below
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the grave yard south of the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a metal tablet bearing the following inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT HAREWOOD
2 CONT
2 CONT ERECTED IN 1771. THE HOME OF COL.
2 CONT
2 CONT SAMUEL WASHINGTON, COUNTY LIEUTENANT
2 CONT
2 CONT HIS BROTHER GENERAL GEORGE WASHINGTON
2 CONT
2 CONT VISITED HERE AND GENERAL LAFAYETTE
2 CONT
2 CONT AND LOUIS PHILLIPE OF FRANCE WERE
2 CONT
2 CONT ENTERTAINED HERE. IN THIS HOUSE 
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES MADISON AND DOLLY PAYNE TODD
2 CONT
2 CONT WERE MARRIED. SAMUEL WASHINGTON
2 CONT
2 CONT DIED IN 1781 AND IS BURIED IN THE
2 CONT
2 CONT GRAVE YARD SOUTH OF THE HOUSE
2 CONT
2 CONT George Steptoe Washington (1770-1809) lived at Harewood, the home of his father, Col. Samuel Washington. His wife was Lucy Payne, a sister to Dolly Payne Todd; and thus it came about that the marriag e of James Madison and Dolly P
2 CONC ayne Todd, in September 1794 took place at Harewood. The officiating clergyman was Rev. Alexander Balmain, a chaplain of the Revolution and for many years thereafter a w ell-known minister of Winchester and the surrounding region.
2 CONC  - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT Info added per DAR's "Lineage Book of the Charter Members" by Mary S Lockwood and published 1895 stating he "was colonel in the Virginia Line of the Revolutionary Army."
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Augustine WASHINGTON b: ABT 1694 in Wakefield, Westmoreland, Virginia, United States 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Mary BALL b: ABT NOV 1708 TO ABT 1709 in Epping Forest, Lancaster, Virginia, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Anne STEPTOE
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: ABT 1766 in Va
