1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 109
2 CONT According to his father's bible he was born ye 13th of Jany. about 2 in ye Morn 1735/6 which by our calendar would be January 24, 1736.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT He married Hannah Bushrod.  The marriage, we assume had taken place at Bushfield only a short time before.
2 CONT Charles Moore inn his Washington chronoloyg, shows that he, then manager at Mount Vernon for his brother George, brought his bride to that place on April 13, 1756.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Westmoreland Co...he in the year 1766, with 114 other patriots, pledged life and fortune in a celebrated protest against the famous Stamp Act; and ten years later he aided materially in the Revolution. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT George Washington married and brought his wife to Mount Vernon early in 1759.  Not long thereafter, probably the same year or the next, John Augustine and his wife Hannah left Mount Vernon and took of their residence at Bushfield.
2 CONT It was a year or two, however, before John Augustine Washington closed up his business at Mount Vernon, for on Sept. 21, 1761, he had a sale at which his brother George purchased several cows and calves, some yearlings, and other 
2 CONC cattle, 16 in all. - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid p. 121
2 CONT From Geo. Washington's diary "About the same time I recd. by express the acct. of the sudden death (by a fit of Gout in the head) of by beloved Brother, Colo. Jno. Auge. Washington."  The day of his death was evidently the 8th or 
2 CONC 9th of the month (Jan.).  A messenger (express), by hard riding, could scarcely have made the distance between Bushfield and Mount Vernon in less than a day and a half.  John Augustine was 51, lacking only a few days.
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - Ibid p. 125
2 CONT Niether John Augustine Washington nor his wife, Hannah Bushrod....has a tombstone, so far as any can now be found at Bushfield.
