1 TITL Captain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was captain of the 3rd Virginia Regiment in 1776 and was killed at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Brandywine '''Battle of Brandywine'''].<br/>
2 CONT DAR# [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_descendants/?action=list&MyPrimary_Seqn=32387&MyLineageCount=1&Control_Min_Seqn=32387 A021605]
2 CONT
2 CONT source: [http://books.google.com/books?id=He4A7-5T_MgC&pg=PA77&lpg=PA77&dq=%22James+william+o%27bannon%22&source=bl&ots=K5EzxWNqHj&sig=JWkgjvUfqIYouOidBVmAe4wfZFY&hl=en&ei=JnekSfOhI4i5nQek4vCkBQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=7&ct=res
2 CONC ult#PPA77,M1 google Books]
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT On Aug 22nd 1739, he was born in Rock Spring in Prince William County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 26, 1767, he was commissioned lieutenant in Captain Morehead's company of militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1775, he organized a company of volunteers from Fauquier County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 29th, 1776 he was commissioned captain in the 3rd Virginia Regiment of the Continental Line. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1776, George Washington's Continental Army was in a tough spot. The British had landed on Long Island in late August and were advancing on New York City. What Washington needed most was more soldiers. Virginia res
2 CONC ponded by sending the 3rd Virginia Infantry, the Old Dominion's first contribution of regulars to Washington's army. Captain John Chilton, a thirty-eight-year-old farmer from Fauquier County, was among those who traveled north wit
2 CONC h the unit. "Great things are expected from the Virginians," he explained to his father-in-law,"and of consequence we must go through great fatigue & danger."
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 11, 1777, with Col. Thomas Marshall commanding, he was killed at the Battle of Brandywine. Col. Marshall's 3rd Virginia Regiment were ordered to take up positions in an orchard at the intersection of what is now Stree
2 CONC t Rd. and Birmingham Rd. About 3 pm on a very hot September afternoon, our William and about 150 fellow soldiers took up positions in this orchard. As the first squad of British Dragoons and Light Infantry approached along Birming
2 CONC ham Road, Col. Marshall's forces were the first to open fire. They were able to stop the British and force them to fall back. Soon after, the British light infantry attacked Marshall's front and left flank. Seeing his situation de
2 CONC teriorate, Marshall ordered his men to fire and fall back. William took cover along with the rest of his regiment behind a low stone fence near the Birmingham Meeting House. They were holding their own, but sustained heavy casualt
2 CONC ies. The British were working their way up Birmingham Hill to the left of Marshall's troops. The British got enough men through that they were able to make an assault and drive Marshall off the hill. The Colonials ordered a retrea
2 CONC t to a second position on a small hill to the rear of Birmingham Hill. Col. Marshall was ordered to pull back after suffering 54% casualties. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A soldier of Captain Chilton's company gave this account of his death: "Captain Chilton realized that he had received a mortal wound, but requested that he should not be borne from the field during the battle, but that he should b
2 CONC e placed against a tree in view of his soldiers. He did this for two reasons, that his men might not be discouraged by the thought that his wound was serious, and because of the small number of soldiers engaged in this battle. Eve
2 CONC n those used for bearing him from the field to a place where his wounds could be dressed, would have been missed." After the battle he was taken to an old Meeting House nearby where he died in a few hours. He had been recommended 
2 CONC for promotion by Col. Marshall a few days before the battle. His sword and other personal effects were brought home by returning soldiers, but his body lies where he fell. His children doubly orphaned, their mother having died bef
2 CONC ore the war began, were reared by his brother Charles and other members of the family. "Rockspring" was eventually sold and passed out of the family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A diary kept by him during greater part of his military campaign recording principal events of the war, is now owned by his great granddaughter Mrs. Eugene MacDonnell of Baltimore; another great-granddaughter, Mrs. C. B. Ryan, of 
2 CONC Norfolk, Va., has a valuable letter written by him to his father in law, Mr. Joseph Blackwell, describing the comparative strength of the British and American forces outside of New York. Accompanying it is a drawing executed by hi
2 CONC mself of the relative position of each army
2 CONT
2 CONT The last entry in his diary reads "Tuesday Sept. 9th, at 2 in the morning we had orders to march, took the road from Newport to Wilington 2 miles then turned to almost north about 2 ms more. we then marched west course 10 miles s.
2 CONC  w. and crossed Brandywine Creek and encamped on the heights of the creek."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:<br/>
2 CONT 1) VA Historical Society biography on Captain John Chilton<br/>
2 CONT 2) Heitman-Historical Register of Officers of the Continental Army (page 154)<br/>
2 CONT 3) Wikipedia - Third Regiment of Virginia Regulars<br/>
2 CONT 4) Ancestry.com - The Battle of Brandywine<br/>
2 CONT
