1 NAME Burgess /Ball/
2 GIVN Burgess
2 SURN Ball
2 _MARNM Ball
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 28 JUL 1749
2 PLAC Bewdley Plantation
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lancaster
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 BURI
2 DATE 1800
2 PLAC Springwood Plantation, Ball Burial Ground  
2 ADDR
3 CITY Leesburg
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 3 MAR 1800
2 PLAC Leesburg, Loudoun County, Virginia
2 ADDR
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Col.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of LIEUTENANT COLONEL.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A005389  DAR Ancestor # A005389]
2 CONT
2 CONT Info added per DAR's "Lineage Book of the Charter Members" by Mary S Lockwood and published 1895 stating he was a "captain of infantry, lieutenant colonel, commanding his own regiment of the line, and volunteer aid-de-camp to Gen.
2 CONC  Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT =============================================================
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT *https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/58072966/burgess-ba
2 CONT *http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=58072966
2 CONT *http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~opus/p53.htm
2 CONT *http://www.kenmore.org/collections/portraits/burgess_ball.html
2 CONT *A Guide to the Ball Family Papers, 1650-1864," Library of Virginia, Accession No. 18770A, http://ead.lib.virginia.edu/vivaxtf/view?docId=lva/vi01103.xml;query=;brand=default
2 CONT *https://www.familysearch.org/wiki/en/9th_Virginia_Regiment_(Revolutionary_War)
2 CONT
2 CONT =========================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgess Ball was born 28 July 1749 at Bewdley in Lancaster County, Virginia, to Jeduthan Ball (1725-1749/50) and Elizabeth Burgess. Ball served as an aide to George Washington (1732-1799), a relative, during the early years of the
2 CONC  American Revolution. He was appointed captain in the 5th Virginia Regiment, then on 17 December 1777, he was appointed lieutenant colonel in the 1st Virginia Infantry. After the war, he moved from Lancaster County to Stafford Cou
2 CONC nty, Virginia, and built Traveler's Rest. By 1793, he had relocated to Loudoun County, Virginia. Ball married first Mary Chichester (1753-1775) 1 July 1770, and they had two children including Burgess Ball (1773-1793). He married 
2 CONC second Frances Thornton Washington (1763-1815), a niece of George Washington, 7 April 1781, and they had eight children. One of their children was Charles Burgess Ball (14 December 1793-2 July 1823) who became a practicing physici
2 CONC an in Loudoun County. He married Lucy Throckmorton Potter (1793-1870) 14 January 1814, and they had five children, including Charles Burgess Ball (17 February 1822-14 April 1883). Charles Burgess Ball represented Loudoun County in
2 CONC  the Virginia House of Delegates in 1849-1850, 1852-1853, 1857-1858, and 1859-1861. He represented the county in the state senate from 1861 to 1865. Ball married Emma Read 27 May 1852, and they had five children. Ball died in Rich
2 CONC mond, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://ead.lib.virginia.edu/vivaxtf/view?docId=lva/vi01103.xml
