1 NOTE !BIRTH - THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 57
2 CONT George Lewis was born in 1757, prob. in  Fredericksburg, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR PATRIOT INDEX - CENTENNIAL EDITION, PART II
2 CONT Gives date of birth as 3/14/1756/7 in VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - Ibid
2 CONT He married Catherine Daingerfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT After residing some years near Berryville he removed to Fredericksburg, and thence to King George Co., dying there at his home Marmion in 1821.  He enjoyed to the fullest extent the esteem and confidence of his uncle, Gen. Washing
2 CONC ton, who bequeathed him a sum of money and one of his swords.
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR PATRIOT INDEX - CENTENNIAL EDITION, PART II
2 CONT Gives date of death as 11/15//1821 in VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE WASHINGTONS AND THEIR HOMES by John W. Wayland, p. 57
2 CONT George Lewis, born in 1757, served with distinction in the Revolution.  In his arms General Hugh Mercer expired after having been mortally wounded in the battle of Princeton.  He was a captain in Baylor's regiment and commanded Wa
2 CONC shington's life guard.  Near the close of the war he married and settled at "Springbury," on the Shenandoah River, 4 miles south of Berryville, now in Clarke Co.,VA., where he was active in affairs of church and state.  In the Whi
2 CONC sky Insurrection of 1794 he was major in command of a body of cavalry.    
2 CONT
2 CONT George Lewis served with distinction in the Revolution.  In his arms Gen. Hugh Mercer expired after having been mortally wounded in the battle of Princeton.  He was a captain in Baylor's regiment and commanded Washington's life gu
2 CONC ard.  Near the close of the war he married and settled at "Springbury," on the Shenandoah River, 4 miles south of Berryville, now in Clarke Co.,VA., where he was active in affairs of church and state.  In the Whisky Insurrection o
2 CONC f 1794 he was major in command of a body of cavalry. - Ibid
