2 DATE 21 APR 1821
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph Ball was a prominent merchant, landowner, industrialist, and capitalist
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Ball was elected Director of the United States Bank at Philadelphia on January 9, 1789.  He was elected President of the Insurance Company of North America.  He was not a soldier in the Revolutionary War, but he helped win 
2 CONC the war by lending money to the United States.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He had no surviving brothers nor sisters and left a great fortune for his relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Joseph Ball married Sarah Lee, dtr of Margaret Brazure and stepdaughter of Col Richard Wescoat.  They had  one daughter, Mary, who married Robert Frazer.
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.archives.upenn.edu/people/1700s/ball_joseph.html
2 CONT
2 CONT * Joseph Ball (died 1821)
2 CONT * University Affiliation:
2 CONT * Trustee 1794-1821
2 CONT * Biographical Summary:
2 CONT * Owner of the Batsto Iron Works
2 CONT * Revolutionary & munitions supplier to the Continental Army
2 CONT * President of the Insurance Company of North America
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Ball was a prominent merchant, landowner, industrialist, and capitalist. His chief industrial pursuit was ownership and operation of the Batsto iron works in Burlington County, New Jersey. He was also an original director a
2 CONC nd president, in 1798-1799, of the Insurance Company of North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the American Revolution he was an ardent patriot, supplying the Continental Army with munitions manufactured at Batsto. After the war he, along with many others in a plan directed by Robert Morris, gave liberally of his gre
2 CONC at fortune in an attempt to restore the public credit. Although this act of charity did not ruin him financially, it did cause him enormous loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ball served as a trustee of the University of Pennsylvania from 1794 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.ststlocations.com/Archives/Donham/Chapter11/
2 CONT
2 CONT (From a transcript of an old newspaper clipping)
2 CONT
2 CONT >For a number of years the officials of Philadelphia have been searching for the lawful heirs to the large landed "Ball Estate" in that city which has been exempt from taxes for 100 years, as it was held in trust by a church organ
2 CONC ization. There have been from time to itme numerous claimants to the estate but as yet none has been able to satisfactorily establish legal lineage as the direct descendents from William, the original owner, who died in 1737.
2 CONT
2 CONT >J.S. DONHAM, of Cory, Clay County, Indiana, and W.R. FITZWATER of Mulberry, Clermont County, Ohio, both claiming to be among the direct heirs to the estate and who for the past 2 years have been making a search for all the other 
2 CONC heirs, have visited Philadelphia and personally examined the records there. They are confident that they, with others, from legal advice, are the only lawful heirs to the real estate in Philadelphia of the value of millions of dol
2 CONC lars, as the direct descendents of Mary BALL. Proceedings, it is said, will be instituted at an early day by these claimants for possession of the property, and later for the possession of the large estate of the son of Joseph BAL
2 CONC L, deceased.   ....
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT #  "Family Empire in Jersey Iron:" by Arthur D. Pierce
2 CONT # "Ball Cousins" by Margaret B. Kinsey. Lamesa, Texas, c. 1980
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * [http://www.batstovillage.org/history.htm A Short History of Batsto Village] "During the Revolutionary War years, Batsto manufactured supplies for the Continental Army. Manager Joseph Ball became owner of Batsto Iron Works in 17
2 CONC 79 ... In 1784, William Richards, uncle of Joseph Ball, became a major owner of The Iron Works."
2 CONT * http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~mcclell2/homepage/richball.htm
2 CONT * http://genforum.genealogy.com/ball/messages/3018.html
