1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Beckley, U.S.A." by Harlow Warren, published 1955.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes and Information from Ruth Beckley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Amelia Neville Craig in February 1785.
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Isaac Craig served in the Revolutionary War.  Details are not known.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Only one of the four daughters to survive childhood. She also was a twin.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Retta Campbell, Neville House Historian, It is doubtful if Neville Island (aka Mountour's Island) went to any of Presley's descendants or had a direct association with them. The island was owned almost entirely by Joh
2 CONC n Neville, then inherited by Amelia Neville Craig, then various parcels willed to several of her children, eventually sold, etc. etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Produce from the island was sold at Pittsburgh on a daily basis and highly regarded. As late as the 1920's, the Waldorf Hotel in New York City had Neville Island asparagus on it's menu. The government bought a large parcel during 
2 CONC the First World War to build a munitions plant, which never did fully operate because the war ended. Unfortunately, this opened the way for industrialization of the island, and by the mid 20th Century it became a smoking, stinking
2 CONC  mess of manufacturers. Most of those are now gone, having been replaced with lighter manufacturing facilities. Those people on the island who have stuck it out are now working for its greenification, and have established an histo
2 CONC rical society. Hidden away on a little hill is a cemetery which once surrounded a church, the building of which was partially funded by Amelia Neville Craig. The church is gone, but several of Amelia's family are still buried ther
2 CONC e, most notably the Fleesons.
2 CONT
