1 NAME George Corbin /Washington/
2 GIVN George Corbin
2 SURN Washington
2 _MARNM Washington
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Oak Hill Cemetery Washington District of Columbia
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 AUG 1789
2 ADDR
3 STAE VA
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 JUL 1854
2 PLAC Georgetown, Washington, DC
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Unfortunate Geni merges with other trees not sourced have invalidated this tree as a reliable source.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Corbin_Washington
2 CONT
2 CONT George Corbin Washington (August 20, 1789 – July 17, 1854) was a United States Congressman from the third and fifth districts of Maryland, serving four terms from 1827 to 1833, and 1835 to 1837. He was also a grandnephew of U.S. P
2 CONC resident George Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington was born at Haywood Farms near Oak Grove of Westmoreland County, Virginia. He attended Phillips Academy and Harvard University, studied law, but devoted himself to agricultural pursuits on his plantation in Maryland. He
2 CONC  resided for the most part at Dumbarton Heights in the Georgetown neighborhood in Washington, D.C..
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington was elected to the Twentieth, Twenty-first, and Twenty-second Congresses, serving three terms from March 4, 1827 until March 3, 1833. In Congress, he served as chairman of the Committee on District of Columbia during th
2 CONC e Twenty-second Congress. He was not a candidate for renomination in 1832, but was elected two years later as an Anti-Jacksonian to the Twenty-fourth Congress, serving one term from March 4, 1835 to March 3, 1837. He was again not
2 CONC  a candidate for renomination.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his service in Congress, Washington became president of the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal Company. He was also appointed by President John Tyler in 1844 as a commissioner to adjust and settle the claims arising under the treaty 
2 CONC of 1835 with the Cherokee Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1852, he was nominated by the Native American Party as a candidate for Vice President on a ticket with Daniel Webster.[1] On Webster's death nine days before the election, the ticket was replaced by Jacob Broom and Reynell Coat
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in the Georgetown neighborhood of Washington, D.C., and is interred in Oak Hill Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=6643760&ref=wvr
