1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Elizabeth “Betsy” Craig (c. 1779–1833)
2 CONT ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Elizabeth “Betsy” Craig (c. 1779–1833) was a pioneer residing in the early Kentucky frontier. As the wife of Isaac Bledsoe, Sr. (1771–1825), she played a foundational role in the settlement of the Woodford and Gallatin County regi
2 CONC ons during the late 18th and early 19th centuries. Her life encompassed the challenging transition from frontier wilderness to established statehood in Kentucky, where she raised a large family during a formative era of American w
2 CONC estward expansion.
2 CONT While her marriage is firmly established by primary legal documentation, her life and lineage are the subject of ongoing genealogical research. Distinguishing her from contemporary women of the same name requires careful cross-ref
2 CONC erencing of marriage bonds and regional land records. As a member of the pioneer Craig family, she was part of the broader migration patterns that defined the settlement of the Ohio River Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Benjamin Craig, Sr. (1751–1822)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Nancy Ann (née Sturman) (c. 1750–1831)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Born: circa 1779, Orange/Essex County, New Jersey
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: March 11, 1833, Gallatin County, Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Isaac Bledsoe, Sr. (1771–1825)
2 CONT Marriage: January 23, 1793, Woodford County, Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT Attributed Children:
2 CONT 1. Lewis Bledsoe (1795–Deceased)
2 CONT 2. Benjamin Bledsoe (1798–Deceased)
2 CONT 3. Merit Bledsoe (1798–Deceased)
2 CONT 4. Lydia Bledsoe (1802–1876)
2 CONT 5. Isaac Bledsoe, Jr. (1805–1842)
2 CONT 6. Elizabeth Bledsoe (1807–Deceased)
2 CONT 7. Watts N. Bledsoe (1812–1872)
2 CONT 8. Job Bledsoe (1816–1887)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Please be advised that while the individuals listed above are traditionally attributed as the children of Isaac Bledsoe, Sr. and Elizabeth Craig, there is currently no primary documentation (such as probate settlements, birt
2 CONC h certificates, or guardianship records) that explicitly confirms these specific parent-child relationships. These listings should be considered speculative until verified by primary source records.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Relevance===
2 CONT Elizabeth’s union with Isaac Bledsoe, Sr. is verified by a marriage bond executed in Woodford County, Kentucky, on January 23, 1793. Her parentage, linked to Benjamin Craig, Sr. and Nancy Ann Sturman, is consistent with regional f
2 CONC amily records; however, definitive proof of this connection typically rests on identifying Elizabeth as a named heir within the Will or estate distribution of Benjamin Craig, Sr. (d. 1822).
2 CONT Unlike her husband, who died intestate and whose estate proceedings are well-documented, records regarding Elizabeth’s life—particularly during the final years of her residence in Gallatin County—are sparse. Her death in 1833 is r
2 CONC ecorded in family histories, though formal death certificates for this era and location were not standardized. Any further verification of her status as the daughter of Benjamin Craig, Sr. should prioritize an examination of the C
2 CONC arroll/Gallatin County probate records associated with his 1822 estate settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT Marriage Bond: Woodford County, Kentucky (January 23, 1793).
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate Records: Estate of Benjamin Craig, Sr. (Carroll County, Kentucky, 1822).
2 CONT
2 CONT Regional Historical Records: References in family lineage databases linked to the Craig and Bledsoe families of Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. Federal Census: Various residency data for Gallatin County (c. 1800–1830).
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Note: Further verification of Elizabeth’s identity and parentage requires primary-source analysis of the Craig family’s estate records and contemporary land deeds to confirm the transfer of property or inheritance between
2 CONC  the Craig and Bledsoe households.
