1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Judge '''James Gilliam Human''' died March 6, 1875 and is buried in 'Humansville's First Cemetery", Humansville, Polk Co., MO. 
2 CONT Husband of Sarah Jane Hopper Human (12 children), Mary Ann Miller Human (1 child), and Emily Miller Human (6 children).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Human-16
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is from a letter or article at the McClung Historical Collection, Knoxville, Tennessee. (Copy sent to Joanne Franklin from Dorothy Zongker, Hutchinson, Kansas):
2 CONT
2 CONT >A FAMILY REUNION OVER 100 GUESTS PRESENT Last Sunday the descendants and relatives of the late Hon. James G. Human held a family reunion at the residence of William Nance, one mile north of this city. There were about 100 persons
2 CONC  present---all bound by the ties of kinship---and this was but about one-third the total number. Judge (James G.) Human was born and raised in Knox County, Tn. He was married to Miss Sarah J. Hopper, of Granger County, East Tennes
2 CONC see, in 1815. (other records say Jan 22, 1818.) Twelve children were born of this union--four of whom are still living. ''Mrs. Elizabeth (Ann Human) Rains, of Cedar County, aged 78 years, is the eldest of the surviving children''.
2 CONC  The others are Captain W. C. Human, of Spokane, Washington; Mrs. Sarah (Human) (George W.) King, who resides near this city; and Mr. J. F. Human of San Marcos, Texas. Judge Human and wife emigrated from East to West Tennessee, th
2 CONC ence to Illinois, and from there to South Missouri. They settled near Bolivar, Missouri (?) and during the winter Judge Human discovered the big spring, of which Humansville is so proud. In the spring of 1834 Mr. Human settled at 
2 CONC the spring, drained the lake which had formed there, and established his home, living for awhile in a tent. Later he built a house about where the residence of William Q. Paxton now stands. At that time the country was inhabited b
2 CONC y the "noble red man"' and wild beasts, such as the boar and panther were entirely too mumerous for safety. Judge Human's nearest neighbor lived 15 miles away. We regret being unable to give more of the early history of this city 
2 CONC and its founder. In 1858 Mrs. Human died. In 1860 Judge Human married Miss Mary Ann Miller. She lived but a year after marriage. One child was born of this marriage--Mr. James Human, of Morrisville. In 1863 Judge Human married Mis
2 CONC s Emily Miller, sister of Miss Mary, and 6 children were born to them, four of whom are living: Mrs. Ida (Human) Lizburn, of this city; Felix Human, of Slagle, One of the triplets born to Mr. and Mrs. Human: Mrs. Mary (Human) Beer
2 CONC s, of Cliquet; and Mr. Pink Human, of Humansvile. Judge Human died March 8, 1875, aged 77 years; a continuous residence here of 41 years. Four generations were represented at this reunion. The oldest present being Mr. King, aged 6
2 CONC 1 years, and the youngest was aged about 3 monmths, being the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. M. Rains. This is the fourth family reunion and each have been largely attended. The rain prevented many from attending, but it was a very e
2 CONC njoyable occasion. Dinner was spread on a large table beneath the trees in the yard, and though ye scribe arrived too late to partake of the good things prepareed for the occasion, the evidences of happiness and contentment everyw
2 CONC here apparent, convinced wus that we missed an opportunity long to be regretted. Lemonade was served and the evening passed away enlivened by laughter and song. Those in attendance from a distance were Mr. Julius F. Human and wife
2 CONC  of San Marcos, Texas; and Mrs. Charles G. Human and son of San Antonio, Texas. Late in the evening the gathering dispersed and each family sought their respective homes feeling that they had visited an oasis in life's desert.
2 CONT
2 CONT [NOTE: There is no date on the above article. The birthyear of Elizabeth Ann Human (Rains) was 1818. She died in 1901. She was 78 years old at the time of the reunion. That would put the date of the reunion at 1896.]
2 CONT
