1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lexington Intelligencer, February 02, 1917
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Sallie A. Chinn Dies at Her Home Near Greenton Last Friday
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Sallie A. Chinn, one of the best known women in Lafayette County,died at the home of her son, Will E. Chinn, north of Odessa, Friday, Jan. 19, 1917.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Harrison County, Kentucky, October 26, 1832, between Paris and Cythiana and she was a daughter of Elder J.A. McHatton, a minister in the Christian church.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of eleven years she united with that denomination and has been a member ever since. When she was seventeen years old she came with her parents to Missouri and settled on a farm near Greenton, where she has since resided
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1850 she was married to Cornelius Chinn, also a Kentuckian. He bought the farm from his father-in-law and lived there until his death a few years later. At the time of his death Mr. Chinn was a judge of the Lafayette County Cou
2 CONC rt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Chinn was left with three small children to maintain and rear to Christian manhood and womanhood, to face the work, and how well she succeeded is attested by the splendid citizenship of her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The living children are Mrs. Ben Porter of Jefferson City and Will E. Chinn of hear here. Another son, James D., died a few years ago at Dewey, Oklahoma. Besides the children there are several grand children and great-grand-childr
2 CONC en left, as well as a multitude of neighbors and friends to mourn "Aunt Sallies" death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held Sunday and was conducted by Rev. J.T. Lawson and interment was in the Greenton Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT A gentle, kind woman of the old school has gone to her reward.--Odessa Democrat.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
