1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_William_Chinn
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph William Chinn (November 16, 1798 – December 5, 1840) was a nineteenth-century politician and lawyer from Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at "Epping Forest" near Nuttsville, Virginia, Chinn graduated from Union College in Schenectady, New York in 1819, studied law in Needham, Virginia and was admitted to the bar in 1821, commencing practice in Lancaster County,
2 CONC  Virginia. He was a member of the Virginia House of Delegates from 1826 to 1828 and a member of the Virginia Senate from 1829 to 1831. Chinn was elected a Jacksonian to the United States House of Representatives in 1830, serving f
2 CONC rom 1831 to 1835. There, he was chairman of the Committee on the District of Columbia from 1833 to 1835. Afterwards, he moved to Richmond County, Virginia where he resumed the practicing law until his death at his estate called "W
2 CONC ilna" near Warsaw on December 5, 1840 and was interred in the family cemetery on the estate along with his wife, Marianne Smith (1802–1865).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT US Congressman. He was born at "Epping Forest" in Lancaster County, Virginia. A student at Union College in Schenectady, New York, he graduated in 1819. After studying law, he was admitted to the bar in 1821 and began a practice i
2 CONC n Lancaster County. He would serve in both the Virginia House of Delegates and State Senate. Elected to Congress as a Jacksonian Democrat in 1830, he served two terms, from 1831 to 1835. After leaving Congress, he moved to Richmon
2 CONC d County, Virginia, where he resumed his profession. He died at his estate, "Wilna", in Richmond County, in 1840, and is buried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/53773157/joseph-william-chinn
