1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wayne was married in Tennessee, but had been married less than a year and a half when his first wife died, leaving him with an infant girl. He remarried 2 months after his first wife's death. At that time he was st
2 CONC ill living in Claiborne County, Tennessee, but later he was living near Mammoth Cave in Knox County when his son, Frank, was born. He moved to the Ab vicinity in Missouri and also lived in Urbana where he operated a water powered 
2 CONC mill. Wayne served in CSA during the Civil war. He enlisted on November 1, 1863, leaving his wife with the children to care for and another one on the way. He served as a sergeant in Company D of the fifteenth Regiment of the Miss
2 CONC ouri Cavalty Volunteers. He was discharged on July 1, 1865. He moved to a farm southwest of Urbana and built a house there in 1874. He was living there when he died. He told his family he wanted to be buried in the corner of Mr. B
2 CONC ullock's field, which is now Mission Ridge Cemetery. Information about the descendents of Andrew Wayne Harrell was given by: Pauline Phariss, 4987 S.E.Princeton, Bartlesville, Oklahoma 74006
2 CONT
2 CONT http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=smithrh&id=I0144
2 CONT Wayne was the son of William and Elizabeth R. Hurst Harrell.  He married Eliza Cunningham on 26 Jun 1847 in Claiborne County, Tennessee.  They had been married less than one and one-half years when Eliza died, leaving him with a b
2 CONC aby girl, Elizabeth Harrell.  He married Malinda J. Bartlett on 05 Jan 1849 in Claiborne County, Tennessee, less than two months after Eliza's death.  By 1852 the family had moved to Knox County, Tennessee, and by 1854 they had mo
2 CONC ved to Indiana.  There were in Urbana, Dallas County, Missouri, by 24 Aug 1860 when the Federal census was taken.  In Urbana, Wayne operated a water-powered mill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wayne served in the United States Army during the Civil War. He enlisted on 01 Nov 1863 and served as a sergeant in Company D., 15th Regiment, Missouri Cavalry Volunteers.  He was discharged on 01 Jul 1865.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1874 they moved to a farm southwest of Urbana and he built a house there; they were still living there when he died.  He told his family that he wanted to be buried in the courner of Mr. Bullock's field.  He was the first perso
2 CONC n to be buried in what is now Mission Ridge Cemetery, south of Urbana, Dallas County, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wayne and Eliza had the one child, Elizabeth, born in 1848.  Wayne and Matilda had ten children:  Mary J., William Frank, Nancy Jane, Douglas, Daniel A., Dora Isabell, Lavesta Cornelia, Charles Andrew, Rosetta, and Josilyn Harrell
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT I thank Pauline Phariss for much of this personal information.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13559842/andrew-wayne-harrell Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Au
2 CONC g 17 2022, 16:30:36 UTC''
