1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George was a farmer. He was in the Civil War and Nancy would have to bury the food to keep the soldiers from eating it from the children. In 1880 they lived in Denton County, Texas. George's branch of the family sp
2 CONC ell their name Herrell. Lived at Grapevine, Texas. See 1870 Census, White River, Benton County Arkansas & 1860 census, Bentonville, Benton County, Arkansas
2 CONT
2 CONT Info provided by: Beulah Hester, RT.1, Box 655, Ada, OK 74820 & Mary Wilson, Box 258, Lone Grove, OK 73443
2 CONT
2 CONT They later moved on to the Indian territiory, stopping for a while at Fort Washita. While crossing the Red River on a ferry, they lost all of their belongings.
2 CONT
2 CONT George W. Harrell listed as a sergeant in Company A, 12th Battalion (Day's), Tennessee Cavalry, M 231 Roll 9. When he returned home he had 17 bullet holes in his clothing. He was wounded in the war when a cannon ball bounced off o
2 CONC f a rock and hit his knee cap.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Garland Lively
2 CONT
2 CONT http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=smithrh&id=I0147
2 CONT Son of William B Harrell and Elizabeth R Hurst.  Husband of Nancy Ann BAKER Herrell.  
2 CONT Father of Henry Levi, Silvester G, Margaret Elizabeth Jane, Mandy, Florence L., Mary Isabell, and William Leander "Lee HARRELL.
