1 OCCU Farmer;  served in Civil War
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The first Harrells originally came from Ireland, landing in Boston. Some of them moved down into Virginia and the Carolinas. My father's parents came on through the Cumberland Gap, landing in Tennessee, near the to
2 CONC wn of Tazwell. My father, Jerome Ruben, was born there in 1835. After he became a young man, he moved on to Buffalo, Missouri, and married Phoeba Anna Hurst, also from Tazwell, Tenn., whom he had known in childhood. They reared a 
2 CONC family of seven children, and in 1884 the family moved to Brown County, Texas; hence from then on my native state until 1930, when my family and I moved to Kentucky, which became my adopted state. "From Missouri to Texas by Covere
2 CONC d Wagon 1884" by James Fred Harrell, MD
2 CONT
2 CONT Reuben's first wife (Lewis) died giving birth to Lafayette Herrell. Layfayette was raised by his grandmother Lewis who moved to Texas in the 1870s. Lafayette's half brother Thomas Huston "Huse" Harrell later joined the Lewis famil
2 CONC y in Texas about 1882. In 1884 when the rest of the family moved to Texas they were all reunited. James Fred Harrell MD.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1884 Reuben Jerome Harrell loaded the remainder of his family into two covered wagons and moved from Dallas County, Missouri to Brown County, Texas. Enroute he stopped off near Grapevine, Tarrant County, Texas and visited for a
2 CONC  while with his brother John James Harrell who had previously settled in Texas. Reuben settled near the Lewis family in Brown County, Texas and tried his hand at cattle ranching. He eventually gave up ranching and went back to far
2 CONC ming. Reuben Jerome Harrell died 5 Jun 1917 in Brownwood, Brown County, Texas and is buried in Lost Creek Cemetery, Brown County, Texas. Phoebe Ann Hurst died 25 Sep 1922 in May, Brown County, Texas and is buried with her husband 
2 CONC in Lost Creek Cemetery. This family seems to have vanished into the dust of Brown County, Texas as I can find no trace of them after 1910. Any assistance would be greatly appreciated. Source: Garland Lively garlandliv@aol.com
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerome Herrell enlisted as a private in Company B, 14th Missouri Cavalry in Dallas County, Missouri. The regiment was disbanded in St. Louis, 3 Mar 1863. Various companies of the regiment were incorporated in other regiments. Jero
2 CONC me then served in Company I, 4th Missouri Cavalry regiment until it was disbanded 13 Nov 1865.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reuben enlisted on 15 Mar 1862 in Dallas County, Missouri at age 23. He was mustered into Company I of Cpt Rice's company 15 Mar 1862 at Springfield Missouri. He was transferred from company B, 14th Cav to Company K. of the 4th Re
2 CONC giment thence to Company I commanded by Captain Rice. He was mustered out 14 Mar 1865 at Warrensburg Missouri. Listed as Jerome H. Herrell. Certified copy of enlistment record # 89779 21 Aug 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a farmer. He went to Texas from Missouri in 1884. Lived near Brownwood, Texas. Info provided by Myra Angst, 13603 Falba Dr. Houston, Texas, 77070
2 CONT
2 CONT http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=smithrh&id=I0148
2 CONT His first marriage was to Rebecca Ann Lewis on Feb. 14, 1858 in Stone County, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT
