1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Military service''': Civil War: member of the Orphan Brigade; 1st Lt of Company I, 2nd Kentucky Infantry - Fort Donelson, Prisoner at Johnsons Island, Battle of Murfreesboro
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1850 - Anderson county, part of, Anderson, Kentucky
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1880 - Magisterial District 1 Lawrenceburg, Anderson, Kentucky, United States
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-40001-737627463/christopher-columbus-lillard-in-familysearch-family-tree FamilySearch Family Tree] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 23 2017
2 CONC , 2:22:19 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher enlisted in the Confederate Army in July 1861. He was mustered in as a first Lieutenant in Company I of the Kentucky Second infantry, the same outfit as William Bond’s that had acquitted itself so valiantly in the Mexi
2 CONC can War. After a stunning victory for Northern forces led by General Grant, the entire regiment of more than eight hundred men was captured.   Among them was Lieutenant Lilliard.  After spending some time in Yankee prison camps, t
2 CONC he Kentucky soldiers were exchanged and returned and fought on at Shiloh and other battles, taking many casualties.  After his release Lillard resigned his commission (2/15/1863) and went home.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Bond quickly realizes that his brother-in-law, also from a whiskey-making family, would make an excellent junior partner.  Thus was born the distilling house of Bond & Lillard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://pre-prowhiskeymen.blogspot.com/2022/05/bond-lilliard-whiskey-born-of-us-wars.html
