1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sarah Mason Grayson (Cooke)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/220880645/sarah-mason-grayson Find A Grave Memorial ID # 220880645]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of John Travers Cooke and Mary Thomson Mason, whom married on 18 November 1784.
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings: (10)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Million Cooke (1785-1842) married Judge John Williams Green
2 CONT * John Travers Cooke, Jr. (7 Feb 1787)
2 CONT * Ann Eilbeck (Nancy) Cooke (22 Feb 1788-1833) m. Sidney Wishart
2 CONT * Elizabeth Travers Cook (1789-1870) unmarried
2 CONT * George Mason Cooke (1792-1866) married Ann Jane Carter
2 CONT * Travers Cooke (20 Sep 1794)
2 CONT * Harriette Carter Cooke ( 5 Sep 1797-1873)
2 CONT * William Cooke (7 Apr 1799)
2 CONT * Hannah Brown Cooke (1 Aug 1801-25 Aug 1826)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Dr. Robert Osborne Grayson, Sr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Robert Osborne Grayson (March 26, 1830-1907), CSA married (1) Mary A. Rachason (1845) on 23 December 1863, Amherst County, Virginia, Married (2) Mary A. Reagan (widow) on 20 September 1877, Culpepper County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Dr. John Cooke Grayson (1832, Stafford County, Virginia, 03 June 1895, Eastern, Culpeper County, Virginia) Married (1) Adelena Frances (Pettus) Walton on 30 June 1868, Prince Edward County, Virginia. Married (2) Georgeanna "Geo
2 CONC rgia" Taliaferro (1841) on 28 December 1886, Culpepper County, Virginia, daughter of Alfred and Hena Taliaferro. Father of Cary Travers Grayson; John Cooke Grayson, Jr. and Sarah Mason Grayson.
2 CONT ____________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850 Census (Alexandria, Alexandria County, Virginia)
2 CONT Sarah M Grayson 55 (nee Cooke) widow
2 CONT Elizabeth T Cook 55 sister
2 CONT Henrietta Cook 48
2 CONT Lucy W Green 24
2 CONT James W Green 27
2 CONT
2 CONT List of Letters remaining in the Post Office, at Alexandria, Virginia, on the 30th day of November, 1854.
2 CONT Sarah Grayson
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1854, Sarah's advertisement of an escaped slave from the farm near Stevensburg, which would be "Salubria."
2 CONT
