1 NAME James T. /McAdams/ II
2 GIVN James T.
2 SURN McAdams
2 NSFX II
2 _MARNM McAdams
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1754
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1833
2 ADDR
3 STAE Alabama
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Capt. in the Revolutionary War, Farmer/Rancher
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James McAdams was born around 1755, probably in Virginia. A family tradition is that the family of James McAdams of the Pendleton District, South Carolina came from Virginia. The profile of this James fits the miss
2 CONC ing son of James and Rebecca McAdams of Orange Co., North Carolina. His oldest son, James, was born in North Carolina in 1773. In addition, his oldest daughter, Rebecca was a name mentioned in the will of Rebecca to her grand-daug
2 CONC hter in Orange Co. James was living in Tennessee in 1780 when his son, Thomas was born. He is listed as serving in the Revolutionary War, in the Virginia 7th and 11th regiments. He appears in the Revolutionary War pay voucher reco
2 CONC rds in the Hillsborough Military District in N.C. in 1780 and 1783, he held the rank of Captain and was issued credits for military supplies. He was issued lands warrents for 400 acres of land in Washington Co., Tennessee in 1782.
2 CONC  He did not stay in Tennessee and moved to the Pendleton District, South Carolina and appears in the 1790 census there. His wife was Jemina, but nothing is known about her. Her maiden name may have been Murray. In 1790, James owne
2 CONC d no slaves, but by 1800, he had acquired six which was a large number for that area at this time. In 1802, 1803, and 1804, James purchased lands on Crooked Creek, a branch of Saluda River. His name appears regularly throughout th
2 CONC e records of Pickens County, S.C. which was located in the Pendleton District. However, the James who appears there after about 1825 was his son, James. Another son, John, who was born in N.C. also remained in the area and died th
2 CONC ere about 1826. The Acts of Congress of 1820 providing additional benefits for war veterans prompted James to acquire 2 separate tracts of land in Ala. He and some of his sons, who were soldiers of War of 1812, received land grant
2 CONC s in Jefferson County, Alabama in 1821. James received a land grant in St. Clair County, Ala. which was signed by, President, John Quincy Adams in 1828. The following year, the document was stolen from a trunk in his house. The th
2 CONC eif was recorded in the County Court House and the deed was never recovered. James wrote a will in 1828 and it was proved in St. Clair Co., Ala in 1833 by his son, Thomas who was the executor of his estate. He names his wife, Jemi
2 CONC na, his 9 children, and 5 of his slaves."
2 CONT
2 CONT --From Book 'SONS OF ADAM- THE HISTORY OF THE MCADAMS FAMILIES'
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT To keep the McAdams families separated, the McAdams Historical Society gave this James the title of  James McAdams Of Pendleton Dist. South Carolina..
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived in Virginia, and North Carolina before moving to Watauga/Washington County, Tennessee where he bought 400 acres in the 1700s and later divided and sold this land, where he and his wife Jemima both signed the deed. They th
2 CONC en moved to Pendleton, SC.
2 CONT
2 CONT The McAdams in Orange Co, North Carolina had the same naming patterns and everyone thought that they were related... But by the Y-DNA Results they are related but distantly. They are know as the McAdams Of Orange Co, NC. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I think another family was in Abbieville, SC and they are also a different family...
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Early Tax List, Orange County, North Carolina, USA - 1779
2 CONT * '''Residence''': He acquired land on Crooked Creek, a branch of the Saluda River., Pendleton District, South Carolina, USA - 1790
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Received a military land grant in St. CLAIR  AL and moved there at an old age., St Clair County, Alabama, USA - Circa 1821
