1 NAME Moncure /Robinson/
2 GIVN Moncure
2 _MARNM Robinson
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 FEB 1802
2 ADDR
3 CITY Richmond
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 NOV 1891
2 ADDR
3 CITY Philadelphia
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU civil engineer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In "The Virginia Free Press" (Charles Town, W. Va.), Thursday, February 12, 1835, page 3, column 2: "Married- On Feb. 2, in Frederick County, by Rev. William M. Jackson, Moncure Robinson, to Charlotte Bennett Rando
2 CONC lph Taylor, only daughter of the late Bennett Taylor."
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moncure_Robinson
2 CONT
2 CONT Moncure Robinson (February 2, 1802- November 10, 1891) was an American civil engineer, railroad planner and builder and a railroad and steamboat owner, who is considered one of America's leading Antebellum period civil engineers. 
2 CONC He was educated at the College of William and Mary and at the Sorbonne where he studied to be a civil engineer, and his most noted project was the Philadelphia & Reading Railroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike many of the engineers of the early nineteenth century, Robinson did not receive his engineering education at West Point. He acquired his engineering education through self-directed study and the observation of engineering p
2 CONC rojects throughout the United States and Europe. Within nine years of the introduction of the first steam locomotive in the United States, he surveyed, supervised the construction, or was the consulting engineer for 721 miles of t
2 CONC rack, or one-third the entire railroad track built to that time. At the time of his death in 1891, over 163,000 miles of track spanned the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with well-known engineers Benjamin Henry Latrobe, II, John Edgar Thomson, and John B. Jervis, Moncure Robinson was in much demand during the late 1820s-40s and after, the transition period from America's Canal Era to the beg
2 CONC inning of the Railway Age. By 1850, the basic technical aspects of the American railroad had been solved by this generation of engineers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moncure Robinson is referred to as "one of the most distinguished civil engineers in the United States" and the "genius of America’s earliest railways." He was instrumental in the early development and growth of the country’s grea
2 CONC t railroad system.
