1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ''''''John A. Washington III, briefly an aide-de-camp to Robert E. Lee during Lee’s first, ill-fated campaign in the Virginia mountains in 1861, known as the Battle of Cheat Mountain.
2 CONT Washington was killed that September while on a reconnaissance mission. He was 40 years old, a widower survived by seven children.
2 CONT He’s remembered today less for his short military career than for two things. One, he was George Washington’s great-grandnephew, and two, he was the last private owner of Mount Vernon. Unable to manage the plantation profitably, h
2 CONC e sold it to a preservation society for $200,000 (about $8 million in today’s money).
2 CONT The Battle of Cheat Mountain ended the war for Washington. For Washington, it meant death. After he was fatally shot, the men with him fled for their lives. When Union commanders learned who he was, they returned his body to Lee u
2 CONC nder a flag of truce. But Union soldiers left something behind at the site, a placard nailed to a tree:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Under this tree, on the 13th of Sept. 1861, fell Col. John A. Washington, the degenerate descendant of the Father of his Country.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet, the Southern papers saw something else entirely. The Richmond Dispatch called him “a blessed martyr in the just and sacred cause.” and so he was.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 'For more pictures, go to the Media section. 
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Washington-1382
2 CONT
2 CONT Also see the Media/Documents section.
2 CONT
2 CONT *John Augustine Washington, III was a Confederate States Army Officer, and great - grandnephew of George Washington. He was also the last Washington to own "Mount Vernon". In 1840 he graduated from the University of Virginia. Thre
2 CONC e years later he married Eleanor Love Selden (1824 - 1860). He purchased a farm in Fauquier County, Virginia called "Waveland" where he made his home. His wife died in 1860 from childbirth. .
2 CONT
2 CONT * Listed as a farmer in the 1850 Federal Census, Fairfax, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Listed as a farmer in the 1860 Federal Census, South West Revenue District, Fauquier, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT *When Virginia seceded from the Union, John volunteered to defend Virginia in the oncoming conflict.  Entered the Confederate Army as Aide de Camp on the Staff of Gen. R. E. Lee, with the rank of Lieut. Colonel. In a letter writte
2 CONC n on September 6th 1861 to his brother-in-law, Dr. W. Fountain Alexander from the "Camp of Valley Mountain" John expresses his concerns of his own survival. "…I don't know when I shall leave this region, or indeed whether I ever s
2 CONC hall do so, as of course my chances are the same as those of other men, and I know some of us will never get away…" signed, "Most Affectionately yours, John A. Washington". He died one week later, killed at Cheat Mountain, W. VA, 
2 CONC while on a scout (sic). He was reportedly shot by a bushwacker.
2 CONT
2 CONT *He had been a close friend of General Robert E. Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * Mt. Vernon by inheritance from his mother, Jane Charlotte, and sold it three years later.  He was the last private owner of the estate of President George Washington. For many years, however, he had shared responsibilities with 
2 CONC his mother for running both Blakeley and Mt. Vernon. To preserve it he sold it to the Mt. Vernon Ladies Association in 1858.
2 CONT
2 CONT * On September 13th, 1861, he was killed at Elk Water on Cheat Mountain, when he reconnoitered too far into enemy-held territory. A distant relative to John Augustine, through the Lees, and a childhood friend, Gen. Lee was hit har
2 CONC d by one of the first personal losses he would experience in the War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT # Stella Pickett Hardy, "Colonial Families of the Southern States of America: A History and Genealogy of Colonial Families Who Settled in the Colonies prior to the Revolution," Baltimore, MD: Southern Book Co., 1958.
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave Memorial # 6045745.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links: http://www.palmspringsbum.org/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I13291&tree=Legends
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=6045745
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth: 	May 3, 1820 Death: 	Sep. 13, 1861
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt. Col. CSA Last private owner of Mount Vernon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	May 3, 1821 Death: 	Sep. 13, 1861
2 CONT
2 CONT Confederate States Army Officer. Great grandnephew of George Washington and the last Washington to own "Mount Vernon". In 1840 he graduated from the University of Virginia. Three years later he married Eleanor Love Seldon. He purc
2 CONC hased a farm in Fauquier County, Virginia called "Waveland" where he made his home. His wife died in 1860 from childbirth. When Virginia seceded from the Union John volunteered to defend Virginia in the oncoming conflict. He serve
2 CONC d as aide-de-camp on the staff of General Robert E. Lee in the campaign of western Virginia. He was commissioned Lieutenant Colonel on this campaign. While reconnoitering in the Cheat Mountains of now West Virginia he was shot by 
2 CONC a bushwhacker and killed. In a letter written on September 6th 1861 to his brother-in-law, Dr. W. Fountain Alexander from the "Camp of Valley Mountain" John expresses his concerns of his own survival. "…I don't know when I shall l
2 CONC eave this region, or indeed whether I ever shall do so, as of course my chances are the same as those of other men, and I know some of us will never get away…"signed, "Most Affectionately yours, John A. Washington". He died one we
2 CONC ek later. (bio by: stars&bars) 
2 CONT
