1 NAME Jane Charlotte /Blackburn/
2 GIVN Jane Charlotte
2 SURN Blackburn
2 _MARNM Washington
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 23 AUG 1786
2 PLAC Rippon Lodge, Prince William, Virginia
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 SEP 1855
2 PLAC Blakeley, Jefferson, Virginia
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==History==
2 CONT * These Blackburn girls, to further complicate the family connections, were orphaned nieces of Mrs. ‘Judge Bushrod’ Washington’s, so Mrs. Washington’s two orphaned nieces married her husband’s two orphaned nephews."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family== *Parents: Richard Scott Blackburn & Judith Ball.
2 CONT Married: 14 Nov 1811 Prince William Co., Virginia to John Augustine Washington (1789-1832).  He was the son of Corbin Washington & Hannah Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Augustine, II & Jane Charlotte (Blackburn) Washington: 	
2 CONT # Anna Maria Washington
2 CONT # George Washington,   b. 1815,   d. Young
2 CONT # John Augustine Washington, III,   b. 3 May 1821, "Blakeley", Jefferson, West Viginia, USA ,   d. 13 Sep 1861, Cheat Mountain, Randolph, Virginia 
2 CONT # Richard Blackburn Washington,   b. 1822
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Liberia==
2 CONT * When her husband died in 1832, he left his entire estate to his wife to be divided as she pleased among her children. So Jane Charlotte became the owner of Mt. Vernon and remained so until the place went to her older son and thi
2 CONC s she confirmed in her will in 1855. The family, as I said, considered Blakeley a summer retreat from the heat and the bad climate of the tidewater area of Mt. Vernon. The family had always been burdened with the responsibility of
2 CONC  slaves, and they felt this burden to be a very serious and unwelcome responsibility, just as George Washington had felt. One thing that they had done from the beginning was to support the American Colonization Society which found
2 CONC ed Liberia, a free nation on the coast of Africa to which they would send freed slaves. The trouble with this project was that it was a very expensive thing to do – to educate slaves to the point where they could be self-sufficien
2 CONC t and then transport them over to Africa and give them enough support to have books, a start to get going and become independent. Judge Bushrod Washington, while he sat on the Supreme Court of the United States, was President of t
2 CONC he American Colonization Society, and the family always supported it. Indeed Jane Charlotte, in her 1855 will, provided for one slave, named William Lyon, to be educated and transported to Liberia if he chose." (A parcel near Clay
2 CONC mont on the 1883 map of the county indicates a parcel owned by a man of the same name)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 	
2 CONT # [S63] Garner Database, J. H. Garner, (compiler), (RootsWeb.com, 2007-02-06), labron00., J. H. Garner Genealogy Database (Reliability: 3). ~Ruth Ritchie & Sudie Rucker Wood, Garner Keene Families of Northern Neck Virginia
2 CONT # [S289] Blackburns in America, W. A. Challacombe, (Carlinville, Illinois: W. A. Challacombe, 1943, 151 pages), page 3 (Reliability: 4).
2 CONT # [S288] The Land Where We Were Dreamin', James T. Surkamp (Editor), (Shepherdstown, West Virginia: Jim Surkamp Presentations, 2003), The Washingtons of Jefferson County (Reliability: 4). by John Augustine Washington, Family Histo
2 CONC rian, Interview 3 Aug 2001
2 CONT # [S536] Lewis of Warner Hall, Merrow Egerton Sorley, (Columbia, Missouri: Printing and binding by E. W. Stephens Co., 1937, 905 pages), page 838 (Reliability: 3).
2 CONT # [S515] Lee, Richard (Col.) Descendants, Cassius Francis Lee & Joseph Packard, Junior, (Boston, Massachusetts: David Clapp & Son, 1872), page 6 (Reliability: 4).
2 CONT # [http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/Search/af/family_group_record.asp?familyid=7803374&frompage=0 	Jane Charlotte BLACKBURN (AFN:12Q1-KVV)] FamilySearch™ Ancestral File v4.19
2 CONT #Find A Grave Memorial # 39976160.
2 CONT
