1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anna Maria Thomasina Blackburn''' was born on 30 Oct 1790 in "[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rippon_Lodge '''Rippon Lodge''']," Prince William, Virginia and died on 21 Sep 1833 in Jefferson Co, West Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT These Blackburn girls, to further complicate the family connections, were orphaned nieces of Mrs. ‘Judge Bushrod’ Washington’s, so Mrs. Washington’s two orphaned nieces married her husband’s two orphaned nephews."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT Parents:<br/>
2 CONT * Daughter of Richard Scott Blackburn and Judith Ball 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # 27 Sep 1810 in "Rippon Lodge," Prince William, Virginia	to Bushrod Corbin Washington (1790-1851) as his 1st wife; he married Maria Powell Harrison 2nd.  He was the son of Corbin Washington & Hannah Lee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	
2 CONT # Hannah Lee Washington (1811-1881).  Married William Pearson Alexander.
2 CONT # Thomas Blackburn Washington (1812-1854).  Married Rebecca Janet Cunningham.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT # "These Blackburn girls and their other sisters were very strong members of the Episcopal Church in its period of revival. The Episcopal Church had gone into a decline after the Revolution because it had seemed associated with th
2 CONC e established church of the colonial government, and therefore with the colonial government and Britain. But it began to come back, particularly under the leadership of Bishop William Meade. Some people became very enthusiastic la
2 CONC y supporters, and the Blackburn women were among them to the extent that Bishop Meade's classic book "Old Churches, Ministers and Families of Virginia," especially refers to these Blackburn girls as 'the first fruits and some of t
2 CONC he most valuable fruits of my early ministry.' 
2 CONT # Provenance: Corbin Washington (1765-1799), son of John Augustine Washington, nephew of George Washington , married to Hannah Lee (1766 to 1801); Bushrod Corbin Washington, son of the above (1790-1851) married Anna Maria Thomasin
2 CONC a Blackburn (1790 to 1833); Thomas Blackburn Washington, son of the above (1813-1854), married Rebecca Janet Cunningham (1820 to 1890) - she, Rebecca, married as second husband Dr Edward Syle; Anna Maria Thomasina Blackburn Washin
2 CONC gton (1854-1909), daughter of the above (ie of Thomas Blackburn Washington and Rebecca Janet Cunningham); married (later Sir) James Alfred Ewing (1855-1935), noted engineer and physicist; the present owner, by descent. [http://www
2 CONC .freerepublic.com/focus/f-chat/2401225/posts 1787 Washington Letter sells for $3.2 million.  The Washington Post, 04 December 2009]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/Search/af/individual_record.asp?recid=26515599&lds=0&frompage=0 Anna Maria Thomasina BLACKBURN (AFN: 12Q1-R9D)] FamilySearch™ Ancestral File v4.19
2 CONT # [S288] The Land Where We Were Dreamin', James T. Surkamp (Editor), (Shepherdstown, West Virginia: Jim Surkamp Presentations, 2003), The Washingtons of Jefferson County by John Augustine Washington, Family Historian, Interview 3 
2 CONC Aug 2001
2 CONT # [S289] Blackburns in America, W. A. Challacombe, (Carlinville, Illinois: W. A. Challacombe, 1943, 151 pages), page 3 (Reliability: 4).
2 CONT # [S515] Lee, Richard (Col.) Descendants, Cassius Francis Lee & Joseph Packard, Junior, (Boston, Massachusetts: David Clapp & Son, 1872), page 6 (Reliability: 4).
2 CONT
