1 NAME Wright R /Simpson/
2 GIVN Wright R
2 SURN Simpson
2 _MARNM Simpson
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Bailey Cemetery, Alton, Oregon County, Missouri, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 14 OCT 1839
2 PLAC Smithville, De Kalb, Tennessee, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 OCT 1913
2 PLAC Alton, Oregon, Missouri, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Thomas James Simpson and Nancy (Moreland) Simpson. Civil War veteran; fighting for the Confederate cause. Married Samantha Cates 05/Jan/1868 at Oregon Co., MO. Married Mary Evelyn Sloan 14/Mar/1897. Judge of
2 CONC  Oregon Co., MO 1896-1906. Farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per MO Death certificate #33671, burial 18/Oct/1913.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~ Judge Wright Simpson died at his home, four miles northeast of Alton, last Friday, October 17, 1913, after an illness of several months, due mainly to advanced age. He was born in Tennessee October 14, 1839, and was 
2 CONC at the time of his death 74 years and 3 days old. He moved from Tennessee to Missouri with his parents when a boy and was reared to manhood on a farm where he has always 1ived. He has been married twice. The first time he was marr
2 CONC ied to Samantha Cates in 1868, who died May 20, 1890; to this union were born 10 children, 9 of whom are living and are Mrs. R.D. 0llar; Mrs. G.W. Bailey; Mrs. J .W. Birkhead; Mrs. Jas. Wiggs; S.M.; Thos.; and Lem Simpson, all liv
2 CONC ing in Oregon County. He was again married to Mrs. Mary Sloan March 14, 1897, who survives him, and to this union was born five children, one dead and four living. The living are Rutha, Solomon, Louis, and Jerusha. He was recogniz
2 CONC ed as a man of strong convictions and as a leader in his community and always championed whatever he considered was for the best interest of the people. In 1895 he was nominated and elected associate judge of the county court, ser
2 CONC ving in this capacity for two years. Again in 1898, he was nominated and elected Presiding Judge of the county court, which position he held for 9 years and to the entire satisfaction of the people. He served in the Confederate Ar
2 CONC my and was in the memorable Price raid. He was also a conscientious, consecrated Christian and was ever ready to defend the teachings of the Bible as he construed it. He will be greatly missed in the community and his demise will 
2 CONC be mourned by his own family and a great concourse of other relatives and friends. His funeral was preached by Rev. E.J. Cates in the presence of a large congregation, Saturday at 3 o'clock and laid to rest in the Bailey graveyard
2 CONC . (obit provided by Nona Williams)* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18683782/wright-r-simpson Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 4 2021, 16:30:24 UTC''
