1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was the son of Commodore Foxall Alexander Parker and Sara Bogardus Parker. 
2 CONT On June 5, 1861 as Daingerfield Parker, he married Amelia Nisbet at the District of Columbia.
2 CONT They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brigadier General Daingerfield Parker was born on May 23, 1832 at New Rochelle, New York, the son of Commodore Foxall Alexander Parker and Sara J. Bogardus Parker. He entered the United States Army just before the Civil War and wa
2 CONC s promoted to the rank of captain. He was in the first and second battles of Bull Run, Antietam, Fredericksburg, Chancellorsville and Gettysburg. He was in command of Fort Slocum during General Early's attacks on the District of C
2 CONC olumbia. He was promoted to the rank of major in the 9th infantry in 1889 and to the rank of lieutenant colonel in 1894 and the grade of colonel in 1896, the year in which he retired. He was promoted to the rank of brigadier gener
2 CONC al, retired by a special act in 1904. After the Civil War, he was in command of the military prison at St. Louis and in command of troops during the opening of the Cherokee strip. He was a member of the Loyal Legion, the National 
2 CONC Geographic Society, Society of the Cincinnati, the Society of Colonial Wars, American Bison Society, Sons of the Revolution and the Metropolitan Club. In 1861, he married Amelia Nesbit of Philadelphia. He died at age 92 on Tuesday
2 CONC , February 24, 1925 at his home, 1431 Twenty-first Street after a brief illness. He was predeceased by his wife and a nephew, Dr. Henry B. Parker who died last week. Survivors included nieces: Miss L.J. Parker and Miss Marion Park
2 CONC er of the District and Mrs. Edward Winder of Baltimore and nephews: Thornton J. Parker of the District; Leroy Parker of Gettysburg, Pennsylvania; Robert B. Parker of Newark, New Jersey and Major William H. Parker of the United Sta
2 CONC tes Marine Corps. Funeral services were conducted at the residence with Rev. Dr. Herbert Scott Smith, rector of St. Margaret's Episcopal Church officiating. Interment was at Arlington Cemetery with full military honors.
2 CONT Source: The Evening Star, Wednesday, February 25, 1925.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/44344687/daingerfield_parker Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 10 2024, 6:23:31 UTC''
