1 OCCU Confederate military officer, attorney
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=10076739
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT George William Chilton, Confederate Army officer, was born to Thomas Chilton and Francis Tribble Stoner on June 4, 1828, in Elizabethtown, Kentucky. He briefly attended Howard College at Marion, Alabama, but with the outbreak of t
2 CONC he Mexican War he enlisted as a private in Capt. Christopher B. Acklin's Company B of Col. John C. Hays's First Regiment, Texas Mounted Rifles. After being discharged on September 13, 1846, he returned to Alabama, where he was adm
2 CONC itted to the bar in 1848 and set up practice in Talladega. Chilton moved to Texas in 1851 and became an attorney in Tyler. The following year he married Ella Goodman of Tyler; the couple had two children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chilton was a fiery orator, a member of the Knights of the Golden Circle, and outspoken in his proslavery beliefs. He favored reopening the African slave trade and believed that every White man should own a Black to perform menial
2 CONC  labor, thus saving the energies of the "master race" for "self-improvement." The owner of five slaves, Chilton believed that a life of physical labor was God's will for the Black race and that slavery was an improvement over a li
2 CONC fe of barbarism in Africa. Chilton was elected as a delegate to the Secession Convention, served from January 28 until February 4, 1861, and then was elected major of Col. Elkanah Greer's Third Texas Cavalry regiment. Samuel Barro
2 CONC n, a trooper in the regiment, said of Chilton that "whenever an opportunity offered, [he] showed himself to be brave and gallant." Chilton commanded a battalion of the regiment at the battle of Wilson's Creek, Missouri, on August 
2 CONC 10, 1861, and received a slight gunshot wound to the head at the battle of Chustenahlah, Indian Territory, in December 1861. When the regiment's one-year enlistment period expired on May 8, 1862, new elections for officers were he
2 CONC ld, and J. J. A. Barker was elected to Chilton's place. For the last year of the war Colonel Chilton served as ordnance officer on the staff of Gen. Hamilton P. Bee.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war he was elected to the United States House of Representatives but was refused his seat by the radical majority in Congress. In 1876 he was a presidential elector on the Democratic ticket. Chilton died in 1883 and was 
2 CONC buried in Oakwood Cemetery, Tyler. He was the father of United States senator Horace Chilton.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Galveston Daily News of Tuesday, Aug. 12, 1884:
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Colonel Chilton (Special to the News)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyler, Aug. 11 - The District Court adjourned today until Wednesday out of respect to the memory of Col. George W. Chilton, a leading attorney, who died last night. Colonel Chilton was an old citizen of Texas and was well and favo
2 CONC rably known throughout this state and Kentucky. He was one of the Hancock electors of 1880. He will be buried tomorrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Galveston Daily News of Wednesday, Aug. 13, 1884:
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral of Colonel Chilton (Special to the News)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyler, Aug. 12 - The funeral of Col. George W. Chilton was the most largely attended of any seen here for several years.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Confederate Army Officer....Private to Colonel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Member of the Knights Golden Circle.
