2 CONT John Q. A. Warren Gravesite
2 CONT Roxton, Madison County, Arkansas, USA 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID
2 CONT 160190796 · View Source
2 CONT MEMORIAL
2 CONT PHOTOS 3
2 CONT FLOWERS 2
2 CONT JOHN QUINCY ADAMS WARREN
2 CONT DIED 7 FEBRUARY 1917 AT ROXTON, MADISON COUNTY, ARKANSAS.
2 CONT Son of William & Elizabeth WILKINSON Warren
2 CONT Dates on Ancestry.com genealogies show John's birthday as 19 April 1829, born in Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was married three times and was the father of 6-7 children. John spent most of his life in Texas but married his 3rd wife, Elizabeth YOUNG Ferry, in Kansas in 1904. They came to Madison County, Arkansas, about 1910. I have fo
2 CONC und the Deed to 102.09 acres of land that he purchased in May 1910. The Deed has a clause in it saying that "at his death or the death of said Elizabeth Warren, his wife, should either survive the other, then this land to become t
2 CONC he absolute property of the survivor in either event." I have also found another Deed dated 16 February 1917 to this land showing that 9 days after the death of John, that Elizabeth sold the land (and moved back to Kansas).
2 CONT
2 CONT It has not been determined why John ended up in Madison County, Arkansas, and no relatives of his have been found here. John died in the Roxton area according to the newspaper articles about his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogers (Arkansas) Democrat, 15 February 1917, from The Madison County (Arkansas) Democrat
2 CONT John Quincy Adams Warren, age about 90 years, was going from Drake’s Creek to his home near Roxton Tuesday afternoon when his clothing caught fire from matches in his pocket that are supposed to have been ignited by fire from his 
2 CONC pipe, which he had placed in his pocket among the matches and he was so severely burned that death resulted the following morning about 7 o’clock. The old man was alone, driving an ox team, as had been his custom ever since he had
2 CONC  resided in this county, and was near the farm house of P. S. (Pleasant) Scott when he discovered his clothing was on fire. He wore a heavy overcoat in addition to his other apparel and had about succeeded in removing all of his g
2 CONC arments when parties who heard his cries for help reached him but his injuries were fatal and the medical aid which was promptly rendered to him could only serve to alleviate his suffering. The deceased was a U. S. soldier in the 
2 CONC war between this government and Mexico in 1845-48, also served as a federal soldier in the Civil war and later rendered valuable service to the United States during the Indian wars of the 70’s. He drew a liberal pension for the go
2 CONC vernment, sufficient for the support of himself and wife, who survives him, but he was not content to be idle and each year cultivated a crop on his farm, using his oxen in preference to horses or mules in drawing his modern ridin
2 CONC g plows and cultivators. His last trip to Huntsville was made last week in a light spring hack to which was hitched his young yoke of oxen, a well matched pair of Jerseys, in which he took great pride.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Huntsville (Arkansas) Republican, 22 February 1917; Roxton (community near Drakes Creek) News Column: Mrs. Elizabeth Warren, the wife of John Q. A. Warren, is leaving us and moving to Kansas to live with her son-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his military records there are affidavits from Dr. N. J. Hill who attended him after his burns until he died the next day, and also from two men who were with him when he died. An 87 year old local man says that his father used
2 CONC  to point to a hill near their farm and say "Old man Warren is buried up there on that hill on the Warren place."
2 CONT
2 CONT Several members of The Madison County Genealogical & Historical Society have visited the area where the Warren farm was located, but a lot of bulldozing and clearing has been done over the past 50 years in that area, so we have be
2 CONC en unable to determine where the Warren house was located and have not found any evidence of his grave which was near the house.
2 CONT
