1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the History of the First Baptist Church of Hillsborough, North Carolina:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elias Dodson was born in Halifax County, VA on October 27, 1807.  He was baptized by his father, Elias Dodson, Sr. into the fellowship of the Mill Creek Baptist Church of Pittsylvania County, VA in May 1832. After he graduated fro
2 CONC m the Academy at Halifax County, VA in 1832, he attended the Baptist Seminary at Richmond (1834-35) and William and Mary College (1836-38). In September 1838, he was ordained a minister in Richmond, VA.  He was involved in the fol
2 CONC lowing ministries:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1844 to 1855 – He was a Missionary Colporteur# for the Beulah Baptist Association of NC.  1856 to 1869 - He was an agent in NC for foreign missions traveling to east TN in 1860. 1869 to 1873 – He was an agent for foreign and domes
2 CONC tic missions in west TN. 1873 – He returned to east Tenn. as agent for Bristol Female College before returning to NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT After returning to NC, he died in Wilmington, NC on December 13, 1882.
2 CONT
2 CONT His last days are described by the Rev. Dr. James B. Taylor in the Biblical Recorder.  Taylor noted Dodson's feebleness when he saw him at the Convention in Warrenton and did not believe he would be able to travel as planned to Wi
2 CONC lmington in December.  But, to his surprise, Dodson arrived on Saturday morning, December 2nd in "a feeble, prostrate condition."  He went to the home of Alexander Oldham, an old friend who lived near the depot.  Dodson was single
2 CONC  and had no immediate family in NC.  The Oldham's took care of him.  He was suffering from diabetes. Dr. Wood was secured to treat Dodson and two nurses were hired by the church to attend to his needs.  A nephew-in-law, Dr. Angle,
2 CONC  arrived a few days before his uncle's death.  Dodson was accustomed to reading 3 chapters of the Bible each day and when he could no longer read, those in attendance read aloud to him.  He died Wednesday at 11:35 AM and it was th
2 CONC e wish of his relatives that he be buried in Wilmington.  The funeral was held at the First Baptist church with a large congregation being present.  The pastors of the Presbyterian and Lutheran churches assisted Reverend Taylor wi
2 CONC th the service.  Dodson was buried in a lot provided by Alexander Oldham.  In his article, Taylor requested that the Baptist of North Carolina, Virginia and Tennessee collect money to erect, without delay, a monument to the memory
2 CONC  of Elias Dodson.12   
2 CONT
2 CONT The NC Baptist Convention raised funds and a monument was placed on Dodson's grave.  However, in 1927 Elias Dodson Poe visited the grave site and reported in the Biblical Recorder that it was in disrepair - overgrown with briars a
2 CONC nd weeds.  The cemetery keeper told him that $150 would repair the monument and grounds and that it was a shame that the grave was not endowed to maintain it properly.  Poe states in the article that Dodson helped establish church
2 CONC es in Hillsboro and Greensboro and that he was a long-time member of the New Hope Baptist church near Statesville.  "He journeyed from the mountains to the sea preaching and stirring up the people in North Carolina for a life time
2 CONC  of service."  Poe implored the First Churches of Raleigh, Greensboro, Statesville, Wilmington and Hillsborough to "honor themselves" by joining efforts to restore Dodson's gravesite.13 It took several years, but in 1934, an artic
2 CONC le appeared in the Biblical Recorder indicating that funds had been raised during the Convention to repair the grave and that E.D. Poe had sent funds that he had raised for this stated purpose, all together $121.80.   The headston
2 CONC e was cleaned, a foot stone placed and the lot endowed for perpetual maintenance. Dodson's grave is located in the Oakdale Cemetery in Wilmington.  The inscription on the headstone (monument) provides only demographic information,
2 CONC  and says nothing about the man and his dedication to planting Baptist churches in towns and villages in NC and TN.  As one writer put it, "It was he who first prominently and persistently brought before the Baptists of the State 
2 CONC the importance of occupying the towns and growing villages.   Not only by his pleadings and contributions, but also with the labor of his own hands, he helped to secure the first house of worship owned by the Baptists of Hillsboro
2 CONC . For a considerable time his plea for the towns seemed almost unheeded, but it bore fruit abundantly during the last eighteen years of his life."14
2 CONT
2 CONT As for the man, here are some excerpts from his obituary published in the 1983 Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Baptist State Convention.14
2 CONT
2 CONT - He was never married.  He cared for a widowed sister and her children with the wisdom and tenderness of a father; but the love of Christ filled his whole heart as the preaching and the service of Christ filled up his whole life.
2 CONC  - As a man his leading characteristic was his genuineness.  Through and through he was real; utterly devoid of sham and pretense. - His most striking mental trait was strong common-sense. - Those who knew him will long continue t
2 CONC o tell smilingly, lovingly, of Brother Dodson's peculiarities.  He was economical in use of words; fond of condensing his thoughts into proverbs and short, pithy sentences.  Few persons ever heard him without carrying away as a me
2 CONC morial of him some of these sayings. - Prayer was to him an unfailing source of strength and joy.  The Bible was to him the voice of God. - His invariable rule was to accept only so much salary as sufficed to meet his ordinary wan
2 CONC ts.  It was a favorite saying of his; "Our imaginary wants are many; our real wants are few." - He entered the ministry in a time of heated theological discussion.  As was natural to him he investigated these subjects thoroughly, 
2 CONC and on all the lines he had sharply defined views which he was always ready to present and maintain.
2 CONT
2 CONT And here are some excerpts about Dobson from the Life and Labors of Elder F.M. Jordan.15
2 CONT
2 CONT - He had a most wonderful memory.  He could repeat almost a whole sermon, that he heard many years ago, and page after page of beautiful poetry, and other literature that he had read. - He was wonderfully accurate in remembering d
2 CONC ates and giving statistics; and I suppose the best historians of his day. - {during a hotly contested meeting} He whispered to me and said, "Brother Jordan, common sense is a very scarce commodity here tonight." - He had no settle
2 CONC d home, and yet he was at home everywhere he went, for almost, if not everybody seemed to love him, and were glad to see him come, and sorry when he left. - {he lost everything at the end of the War} And then at the close of the w
2 CONC ar some Yankees met him in the road and took his watch away from him..... You can imagine how he felt ... lost everything. {Shortly thereafter} I asked him to preach. He took his text, "Lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven." 
2 CONC  ... Said all that he had saved, he had lost, and all that he had given away he had saved.  "Thank God," said he, "it is in heaven now where the Yankees can't get it." - He asked a good woman if she would have him.  She refused.  
2 CONC "Well," he says, "will you take the Biblical Recorder?" ... The Recorder never had a stronger friend or advocate. - He generally rode an inferior horse.  On one occasion he met a little boy, who asked him where he was going.  The 
2 CONC old man replied, "I am a follower of the Lord Jesus Christ."  "Well," the little fellow says, "you will have to get a better horse than this, or you will never overtake Him." - Brother Dodson took an active part in establishing Ba
2 CONC ptist preaching and Baptist churches in towns.  He said Paul did not neglect the towns. - He wore a wig.  I have slept with him time and again, in the same room, and never saw him take off his wig, nor put it on; and yet he took i
2 CONC t off every night.... His head was as bald and smooth as a peeled onion.  I saw it one time.
2 CONT
2 CONT What an honor it is for our church to have such an intelligent, devoted and beloved man as part of our history and legacy.  He truly is the "earthly father" of First Baptist Church, Hillsborough, NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.fbchillsborough.org/content.cfm?id=457
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT [note: it turns out that Rev. Elias Dodson was 4th cousin to B. F. Stamps.  B.F. Stamps could not have had all the genealogical records to determine proper relation.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ELIAS DODSON By B. F. Stamps
2 CONT
2 CONT    My father was a first cousin to Elias Dodson.  In response to a brief notice in the Biblical Recorder, I have received several letters giving quite a lot of information about this remarkable man.  “Last but not least” is the fo
2 CONC llowing from A. C. Dixon.
2 CONT     When I was a boy I used to read brief articles from E. Dodson in the Tennessee Baptist and other papers. Perhaps some reader of the Baptist and Reflector has kept a file of these, many of which are worthy of being placed in pe
2 CONC rmanent form.  I am sure A. C. Dixon’s letter will interest your readers.
2 CONT    “Metropolitan Tabernacle, Newington Butts, London, S. E.
2 CONT
2 CONT          Sept. 24, 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Rev. B. F. Stamps, Dover, Tennessee, U.S.A.
2 CONT     My dear Bro. Stamps: I remember Elias Dodson as one of the best men I ever knew. His annual visits were a benediction to me in my first pastorates, and I learned much rom him, not only from books, with which he was so familiar
2 CONC , but his Christian character made a great impression on me. After his death his diaries were sent to me in Baltimore, and I thought I might write a brief biography. But, in the pressure of work, I failed to do so.
2 CONT    At the request of Rev. C. T. Bailey, at that time editor of the Biblical Recorder, in Raleigh, I returned the diaries to him. I suppose they are now in the possession of the biblical Recorder. They are well worth preserving, an
2 CONC d I believe that a good, interesting and useful book could be made out of them. I wish that you would get hold of them and put them into readable shape.
2 CONT         Ver cordially yours,
2 CONT                   “A. C. Dixon”
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptist and Reflector (Nashville, Tennessee)
2 CONT  Thursday, February 18, 1915
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ELIAS DODSON AGAIN.
2 CONT
2 CONT    For inasmuch as many have taken in hand to write of this unique character, it seems good unto me also to add a few feeble remarks. My father’s father and Elias Dodson’s mother had the same father and mother.  He was never marri
2 CONC ed.  Therefore if the Irishman’s description of his can be true, I am a descendant of Elias Dodson. Pat said of his cow: “She will give milk always and never have a calf. It runs in the stock. Her mother before her never had a cal
2 CONC f.”
2 CONT    Elias Dodson once fell in love with a young lady. For some cause or other he did not relate this fact to her. But he got my uncle, T.H. Stamps, to go with him to see her and give him his opinion of her. Instead of telling Elias
2 CONC  Dodson what he thought of her, he told the young lady. He never thought any less of my uncle for marrying his sweetheart. In fact, he commended my uncle for agreeing with his estimate of her many excellences. Of this union only o
2 CONC ne child reached his majority. That was J. J. Stamps, a Baptist minister, who died in Knoxville a few years ago. My uncle was captain of a battery in Lee’s army. His chaplain was worthless, and my uncle had to preach to his men. S
2 CONC oon after the war he was ordained. After his wife’s death, a few years after the war, he came to stay with my father in Kentucky. He came in possession of a part of the library of Elias Dodson. Thus I had the privilege of reading 
2 CONC some of his valuable books. But he had in his possession in manuscript a history of his mother’s family, back to Wales, where they were Baptists so far back that the memory of man runneth not to the contrary.
2 CONT    I suggest that W. D. Upshaw, whose mother was a Stamps, be appointed a committee of one to find the manuscript and publish it. While the “Short and Simple Annals” of the family might not be a very exalted theme, yet two such wr
2 CONC iters as Elias Dodson and W. D. Upshaw could pay such tributes to their mothers that every page of the volume would sparkle with interest.
2 CONT     I have space for but one or two unpublished incidents of this remarkable man. At one time he was employed to work as missionary in North Carolina at a salary of $600 a year.  With this money he employed two other missionaries 
2 CONC at $300 each, and worked as missionary, paying his own expenses out of his own private funds. At one time, while missionary in North Carolina, he went to a county-seat that had no Baptist church. The courthouse and lot were advert
2 CONC ised for sale at public auction. He attended the sale and bought the property for a Baptist church. A short time afterwards he officiated at a wedding at the Governor’s mansion. When the guests learned that he was the man who had 
2 CONC bought the courthouse for a Baptist church, they made up the amount and handed it to him. He thanked them for the contribution, stating that he was not at all surprised. He was doing business for the Lord, and he fully believed th
2 CONC e Lord would pay back to him the money he had paid for the house.
2 CONT    If the history of this remarkable man could be written, it would be very interesting. I imagine a book written by him would be no less interesting.             B. F. STAMPS.
2 CONT    Dover, Tenn.
2 CONT
2 CONT _________
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptist and Reflector (Nashville, Tennessee) 18 June 1914, Thursday
