1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sidner was living at home in Hardin County, Kentucky, in 1850. When the 1860 Henry County, Missouri, was taken, Sidner, Hannah, Letitia, and William were living with the Martin Snow family. Both Sidner and Martin S
2 CONC now were listed as tanners. 
2 CONT       The 1870 Watonwan County, Feildon Township, Minnesota, census (p. 550) lists Sydney Reno, 40 b. KY, farmer; Hannah M. 31, b. KY; Letticia J. 13, b. IL; Albert M. 9, b. MO; John T. 8, b. MN; Sidney G. 5 b. IL; and Judson 9/12
2 CONC  b. MN. 
2 CONT       The 1880 Maysville, DeKalb County, Missouri, lists Sidney Reno 50, b. KY; Martha 24, b. OH; John 17, b. MN; Sidney 15, b. IL; Judson 10, b. MN, Mary 6, b. MO; William 3, b. MO; and Christopher 7/12, b. MO. 
2 CONT       The 1900 Sheridan County, Nebraska, census (Beaver Precinct, p. 185) lists Sidney Reno, Dec. 1829, age 70, b. KY; William, Apr. 1877, age 23, b. MO; and Effie, daughter-in-law, May 1881, age 19, b. IN. 
2 CONT       The 1910 Sheridan County, Nebraska, census (Milan Precinct, p. 26A) lists A. J. Moss, 38 b. IN, married 1 time for 16 years, parents b. IN, farmer; Mary, wife, 36 b. MO, married 1 time for 16 years, no children, father b. KY
2 CONC , mother b. OH; and Sidney Reno, father-in-law, 80 b. KY, widow, parents b. KY. 
2 CONT       [Steve Fancy research] He enlisted in the 11th Minnesota Infantry, Company B, August 6, 1864, and was discharged June 26, 1865 at Gallatin, TN (pension record dated Jan. 2, 1915, Pattonsburg, Missouri). His pension file#6596
2 CONC 05 dated January 15, 1898, from Shannon County, South Dakota, listed his wives and children and their birth dataes. Based on the birthplaces of his children, he moved his family from Kentucky to Daviess County, Missouri, between 1
2 CONC 857 and 1860. According to the Nebraska publication "Beaver Valley and its People," 1970, "In 1887 Sidney Reno, Albert Reno's father, came to Nebraska from Missouri bringing with him his other three children, William, Cordellia, a
2 CONC nd Judson. He filed on a 160 acre homestead which lay in the vicinity of the buttes, known as The Pass. Mr. Reno was a widower." His pension file contains a document dated May 7, 1891, from Adaton, Sheridan County, Nebraska, where
2 CONC  he was living, saying that since leaving the service he had resided in Minnesota, Missouri, and Nebraska. One document in his pension file says that after the Civil War he lived in Farmington, Dakota County, Minn., to 1866; Waton
2 CONC won Co., Minn. 1866-1870; then Santa Rosa, Daviess Co., Missouri 1870 to 1885; then Adaton, Sheridan Co., Neb. February 1885 to about 1899; then in Dent Co., Missouri 1899-1909; then Nebraska until 1914; and finally Pattonsburg, M
2 CONC issouri. His October 28, 1914, document was witnessed by Ellen Reno, Christopher's wife.  http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/d/a/m/Sue-Reneau-Damewood/BOOK-0001/0004-0123.html#CHILD617
