1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hulda Elizabeth Hawkins Keene 
2 CONT BIRTH	18 Feb 1835 Palmyra, Marion County, Missouri, USA 
2 CONT DEATH	2 Jun 1918 (aged 83) Bonham, Fannin County, Texas, USA 
2 CONT BURIAL	 New Hope Cemetery Ivanhoe, Fannin County, Texas
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/6416944
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulda Elizabeth Hawkins was married to Samuel Louden Keene. They were married: August 20, 1853 by F.G. FAWCETT Ordained Minister of the Gospel FANNIN COUNTY, TEXAS 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents were: Strother Moses Hawkins and Francis Lucinda RENEAU/Reno. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brothers and sisters were:Mary Francis, Hawkins,James S. HAWKINS,Joseph S. (Joe) Hawkins,Strother Moses (Jr.) Hawkins,Elizabeth Jane Hawkins,Samuel (Sam) W. R. HAWKINS
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary-Fannin County Paper, June 7, 1918: "Funeral of Mrs. Hulda Keene- Yesterday afternnon the funeral of Mrs. Hulda Keene, who died here Sunday afternoon at 5 o'clock, was held at the Baptist Church in Ivanhoe, Rev. Fount Jone
2 CONC s of Gainesville, conducting the services. There was probably never before in the history of Ivanhoe so great a gathering at a funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT This woman was an honored pioneer of that community. For her father Hawkins Prairie was named many years ago. She herself was the last  charter member of the old Sandy Creek church, which years ago was known all over this section 
2 CONC of Texas, and which counted among its members, some of the greatest and best of our early pioneers. It was moved to Ivanhoe some years ago, and is now known as the First Baptist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the services were concluded, the body was taken to the New Hope cemetery and buried. People from all over the river section and from many other parts of the county were present.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulda E. Hawkins was born in Marion County, Mo. eighty-three years ago. Her father came to this county in a very early day, and in 1853 she was married to S.L. Keene in the community in which she was buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT They reared a large family, she being the mother of thirteen children, eight of whom are living, as follows: Dr. Pres. Keene of Shawnee, Okla., Rev. R. L. Keene of this city, Walter and Sam Keene off Ivanhoe, Marion Keene, Mrs. W.
2 CONC  L. Blake of Olney, Mrs. R. H. Skipworth of Stamford, and Mrs. C. E. Taylor of this city. The latter daughter lived with her mother, and for years has cared for her as tenderly as if she were a child, her mother having been a help
2 CONC less invalid for the past four years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her own children, Aunt Huldah reared nine orphans, one of whom is a colored woman, who was one of the sincere mourners at her funeral. Besides her children, Aunt Huldah is survived by about sixty grandchildren and a
2 CONC bout 54 great grandchildren. She was a mother to that whole river country where she lived so long, and it was to Aunt Huldah they all went when they wanted comfort or advice and help. Hers was a great heart and it found a place in
2 CONC  it for the sorrows of the distressed as well as the joys of the happy. Therefore her own heart was made richer, and her own life made happier. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Huldah was a faithful member of the Baptist Church, and one of its staunchest supporters. Her life blessed others as they now bless her."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fannin County Paper
2 CONT June 7, 1918
2 CONT Funeral of Mrs. Hulda Keene
2 CONT Yesterday afternoon the funeral of Mrs. Hulda Keene, who died here Sunday
2 CONT afternoon at 5 o'clock, was held at the Baptist Church in Ivanhoe, Rev. Fount
2 CONT Jones of Gainesville, conducting the services. There was probably
2 CONT never before in the history of Ivanhoe so great a gathering at a funeral.
2 CONT This woman was an honored pioneer of that community. For her father
2 CONT Hawkins Prairie was named many years ago. She herself was the last
2 CONT charter member of the old Sandy Creek church, which years ago was
2 CONT known all over this section of Texas, and which counted among its members,
2 CONT some of the greatest and best of our early pioneers. It as moved to
2 CONT Ivanhoe some years ago, and is now known as the First Baptist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the services were concluded, the body was taken to the New Hope
2 CONT cemetery and buried. People from all over the river section and fron many
2 CONT other parts of the county were present.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hulda E. Hawkins was born in Marion County, Mo. eighty-three years
2 CONT ago. Her father came to this county in a very early day, and in 1853
2 CONT she was married to S.L. Keene in the community in which she was buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT They reared a large family, she being the mother of thirteen children, eight of
2 CONT whom are living, as follows:
2 CONT Dr. Pres. Keene of Shawnee, Okla., Rev. R. L. Keene of this city, Walter and
2 CONT Sam Keene off Ivanhoe, Marion Keene, Mrs. W. L. Blake of Olney, Mrs.
2 CONT R. H. Skipworth of Stamford, and Mrs. C. E. Taylor of this city. The latter
2 CONT daughter lived with her mother, and for years has cared for her as
2 CONT tenderly as if she were a child, her mother having been a helpless invalid
2 CONT for the past four years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her own children, Aunt Huldah reared nine orphans, one of
2 CONT whom is a colored woman, who was one of the sincere mourners at
2 CONT her funeral. Besides her children, Aunt Huldah is survived by about
2 CONT sixty grandchildren and about 54 great grandchildren. She was a mother
2 CONT to that whole river country where she lived so long, and it was to Aunt Huldah
2 CONT they all went when they wanted comfort or advice and help. Hers was a
2 CONT great heart and it found a place in it for the sorrows of the distressed as
2 CONT well as the joys of the happy. Therefore her own heart was made richer, and her
2 CONT own life made happier. Aunt Huldah was a faithful member of the
2 CONT Baptist Church, and one of its staunchest supporters. Her life blessed
2 CONT others as they now bless her.
2 CONT
