0 @N6000000004047135384@ NOTE The oldest of four children; source: Personal viewing, "George Washington," Vol. One, Young Washington, Douglas Southhall Freeman, Pub. 1948.
0 @N6000000003616066891@ NOTE http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/r/i/James-A-Price/GENE5-0009.html
0 @N6000000003615408664@ NOTE Residence: "Forest Plantation", now "Epping Forest" Lancaster Co,VA Event: Vestryman Unknown "St. Marys White Chaple",Lancaster Co,VA Event: 800 acres of the "Sherwood Forest" land grant Unknown 1680 Stafford Co,VA Note: 
1 CONT Col.Joseph I Ball is the grandfather of U.S. President George A Washington.
1 CONT
1 CONT m. (2) Mrs. Mary Johnson, 1707/8, mother of Mary.
1 CONT returned to Virginia 1677.
1 CONT
1 CONT 25 JUN 1711 probated July11, 1711 
1 CONT Reference Number: 188 
1 CONT Note: 
1 CONT 1. This Joseph is widely acceptedas the probable father of Mary Ball, mother of George Washington. This had been very questionable in mymind because Colonel William Ball would have been at least 63 years old at thetime of conceivi
1 CONC ng the father of Mary and even older when fathering a second son named Joseph [tradition is that Colonel William Ball married a widow, Mary (various surnames), at age 63 and had Mary and the secondJoseph (Joseph not confirmed)]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT I had previously recorded this Joseph's brother, Captain William Ball, as the father of the Joseph Ball (wife Mary Spencer) as the father of George Washington's mother. However, I now have joined the supporter's that this Joseph i
1 CONC s the maternal grandfather of George Washington. Information that Mary'sfather "died early in her life" and "by 13 her mother was also dead" and otheraccepted data, e.g., the name and other husbands of this Joseph's wife and whoth
1 CONC e other Joseph Ball was, lends acceptance to what is recorded herein.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. Joseph's will was dated June 25, 1711 and probated July 11,1711. He was a civil Lieutenant Colonel of Lancaster County, Virginia; staunchand liberal supporter of the Established Church; and a Vestryman.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT N1363 [ Joseph BALL] 
1 CONT The house at Epping Forest in Lancaster Co., VA was built in 1680 by Col. Joseph Ball. He named it after the Ball family estate in England. He gave the farm property and house to his son Joseph, Jr., and divided his personal prope
1 CONC rty among all five of his children. He reserved the right to live in the manor until his death; shortly thereafter, he married again, to Mary Montague Johnson.
0 @N6000000003484295164@ NOTE (Research):>Birth note: Birth Surety:2
1 CONT
