0 @N6000000002906180279@ NOTE Need clarified data on ancestry.  See Kirk Q. Bigham, Major Abraham Kirkpatrick and His Descendants,  (1911), 15, "The maternal line was equally illustrious, as appears from the histories of the Virginia families in the Congressio
1 CONC nal Library: Mary Ann Oldham was a second cousin to George Washington, being the daughter of Anne Conway, who was a daughter of Ann Ball, who was a sister of the half blood to Mary Ball, the mother of George Washington."  See also
1 CONC  Ambrose Winston Deatrick, "Society of the Cincinnati Affidavit of Descent," 4 March 1940, 1-2, "Military Record:  Second Lieutenant 12th Virginia, December 1776; First Lieutenant 12th Virginia, April 2nd, 1777; Regiment designate
1 CONC d 8th Virginia, 14th September, 1778; Captain -----, 1780; Killed at Eutaw Springs, 8th September, 1781.  Authorities for Military Record:  see "Historical Register of Officers of the Continental Army" by F. B. Heitman.  Biographi
1 CONC cal sketch:  Conway Oldham was the son of John Oldham and Anne Conway.  Anne Conway was the daughter of Col. Edwin Conway and Anne Ball.  Anne Ball was a half sister of Mary Ball, mother of George Washington.  (Conway's mother was
1 CONC  a half cousin of George Washington).  Authorities of Descent:  "The Compendium of American Genealogy;" "First Families of America," Volume 6, page 185, issue of 1937 (also see page 384 and 387)."
1 CONT
1 CONT Jason E. Barton, The Barton Family, 1970.  p1-2. DAR Library.  "In 1771-72 they moved from Fauquier County, Virginia, to Surry County, North Carolina.  David Barton was killed by Indians about 1777 while on an expedition into Kent
1 CONC ucky, possibly in a party with Daniel Boone.  Ruth Oldham was the daughter of John and Ann (Conway) Oldham."  
1 CONT
1 CONT 1766: Clara McSage and Laura Jones, eds., Early Records - Hampshire County, Virginia,  (Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1969), "Laurence Ross m. Susan Oldham, daughter of (John?) dead 1766 and Ann Conway Oldham(?) who,
1 CONC  a widow by 1766, married Darby Aughney."
1 CONT
1 CONT 1796 WILL: Ann Conway Oldham,  "a widow by 1766, married Darby Aughney." (McSage & Jones, supra).  Her will, probated 15 April 1796, names children Samuel Oldham [who was the heir-at-law for brother Conway Oldham, killed at the Ba
1 CONC ttle of Eutaw Springs, South Carolina on Septemer 8, 1781], Winifred Neville [wife of John], Abigail Lyle [wife of John], Susannah Ross [wife of Laurence], Mary Ann Kirkpatrick [wife of Abraham], Nancy Rector [wife of Daniel, Sr.]
1 CONC , and grandchildren Samuel Bray [not yet matched to a parent], Nancy Rector [daughter of said Nancy and Daniel], Conway Rector [son of said Nancy and Daniel].  The transcript of the attested copy is in the collection of the Filson
1 CONC  Club, Louisville, Kentucky, 4 March 1936:  
1 CONT      "IN THE NAME OF GOD, AMEN, I Anna Aughey, of the County of Hampshire and State of Virginia considering the uncertainty of this mortal life and being of sound and perfect mind and memory, blessed by Almighty God for the same, 
1 CONC do make and publish this my last will and testament in manner and form following, that is to say:
1 CONT      First, I give and bequeath to my grand-son, Samuel Bray, the following slaves by name Peg, a woman, Peter, a boy, Rachel, a girl, and Mary, a girl and also I give and bequeath unto my beloved son, Saml. Oldham, the sum of one
1 CONC  shilling sterling, Also I give and bequeath to my daughter, Wineford Nevil the sum of one shilling sterling.  Also I give and bequeath to my daughter Abigil Lyal one shilling, Also I give and bequeath to my daughter, Susannah Ros
1 CONC s, one shilling.  Also I give and bequeath to my daughter, Mary Ann Kirkpatrick the sum of one shilling and also I give and bequeath to my daughter, Nancy Rector the sum of one shilling and lastly as to all the rest, residue, and 
1 CONC remainder of my house-hold and kitchen furniture and of my personal estate, goods, and chattels of what kind or nature soever that I may possess at my decease, it is my will that they be equally divided acording to quallety and qu
1 CONC antity between my two grand-children, Samual Bray and Nancy Rector, and also I do hereby appoint Conway Rector and Samuel Bray, sole Executors of this my last will and testament, hereby revoking all former wills by me made.  In wi
1 CONC tness whereof, I have hereunto set my hand and seal the seventeenth day of November in the year of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and ninety five.
1 CONT                                                         her
1 CONT                                                     Anna    Aughney (Seal)
1 CONT                                                         mark.
1 CONT Signed, Sealed, Published, and Declared by the above named Anna Aughney to be her last will and testament of us the subscribers.
1 CONT                                         George Currulkers, Daniel McLaughlen,
1 CONT                                         William Young, John Higgins.
1 CONT
1 CONT At a Court held for Hampshire County the 15th day of April, 1796, this last will & testament of Ann Aughney, deceased, was proved by the oaths of William Young and John Higgins two of the witnesses thereto and is ordered to be rec
1 CONC orded.
1 CONT                                         Test.  And. Wodrow, Cl. Cur.
1 CONT
1 CONT A TRUE COPY
1 CONT     Attest:  R.C. Stewart, Clerk
1 CONT              Cty. Ct. Hamp. Co., W.Va.
1 CONT     Will Book No. 1-22, pg. 51."
1 CONT copy of will  provided by Jeanne W. Jackson, 772 Apple Ave., Ukiah, CA 95482
1 CONT
1 CONT ADDITIONAL SOURCES:  W.H. Miller, "Miller, Woods, Harris, Wallace, Oldham" et. al, (1907). p587.  Also, Todd G. Oldham, World Family Tree #479;  Jeanne W. Jackson 1986 notes; Carolyn Hale.
