0 @N6000000002906278910@ NOTE Ambrose Winston Deatrick, "Society of the Cincinnati Affidavit of Descent," 4 March 1940, 1-2, "Military Record:  Second Lieutenant 12th Virginia, December 1776; First Lieutenant 12th Virginia, April 2nd, 1777; Regiment designated
1 CONC  8th Virginia, 14th September, 1778; Captain -----, 1780; Killed at Eutaw Springs, 8th September, 1781.  Authorities for Military Record:  see "Historical Register of Officers of the Continental Army" by F. B. Heitman.  Biographic
1 CONC al sketch:  Conway Oldham was the son of John Oldham and Anne Conway.  Anne Conway was the daughter of Col. Edwin Conway and Anne Ball.  Anne Ball was a half sister of Mary Ball, mother of George Washington.  (Conway's mother was 
1 CONC a half cousin of George Washington).  Authorities of Descent:  "The Compendium of American Genealogy;" "First Families of America," Volume 6, page 185, issue of 1937 (also see page 384 and 387)."
1 CONT
1 CONT 20 April 1781 BATTLE OF SECOND CAMDEN:  M. Lee Minnis, The First Virginia Regiment of Foot 1775-1783,  (Willow Bend Books, 1998), 99-100, "Greene arrived at Camden on 20 April [1781] with his some 1,400 men... Those in the First V
1 CONC irginia known to have been in the battle [of Hobkirk Hill, also known as Second Camden] were...Capt. Conway Oldham... The Second Battle of Camden was fought at Hobkirk Hill which is now a handsome residential area.  Markers at the
1 CONC  intersection of U.S. 521 and 601 (Broad St.) point in the direction of Greene's headquarters and the American line just north of Greene St.  The field of battle is obliterated with homes, some of which are historic, though of a l
1 CONC ater period."
1 CONT
1 CONT May/June 1781 Siege of Ninety-Six:  M. Lee Minnis, The First Virginia Regiment of Foot 1775-1783,  (Willow Bend Books, 1998), 101, "Some of those of LCol. Richard Campbell's First Virginia in the Siege [of May/June 1781 Siege of N
1 CONC inety-Six, South Carolina] were ... Capt. Conway Oldham... Greene had lost 57 killed, 70 wounded and 20 missing while the Loyalists lost 27 killed and 58 wounded."
1 CONT
1 CONT Minnis, M. Lee. The First Virginia Regiment of Foot, 1775-1783. [Lovettsville, Va.]: Willow Bend Books, 1998.  http://worldcat.org/oclc/39355347.  Oldham, Conway, Captain.  "Oldham was in the Eighth Virginia as of 14 September 177
1 CONC 8 [Gwathmey 1987, p594].  Pvt. Samuel Harris reported that he joined Capt. Oldham’s Company on 7 October 1780.  The company became part of a regiment of militia commanded by Maj. Ridley and fought in the Battle of Guilford Courtho
1 CONC use.  Maj. Ridley was ousted for disobedience of orders in thtat battle and the regiment was absorbed into LCol. Richard Campbell’s First Virginia.  The battles of Hobkirk Hill (2nd Camden) and Ninety-Six followed in that regiment
1 CONC .  Capt. Oldham was killed at Eutaw Springs [Selected Records, Rol 402, S10,813].  His land warrant went to Samuel Oldham, heir-at-law [County Records 1993, p35]."
1 CONT
1 CONT Greene, George Washington. The Life of Nathanael Greene, Major-General in the Army of the Revolution. New York: Hurd and Houghton, 1871, 352:  Captain Conway Oldham of the 1st Virginia regiment is accused of insubordination. The c
1 CONC ourt acquit him with honor. " The General disapproves, and for the very great respect he has for Captain Oldham,
1 CONT as a good officer, he is sorry he cannot view his conduct in the same innocent light the court did. Subordination and respect from inferior to superior officers is so necessary in the very existence of an army, that the General is
1 CONC  surprised that an officer should betray the least symptoms of a want of either, especially as the only sure way of enforcing obedience from inferiors, is to set the example by the conduct to superiors. No army can hope to be usef
1 CONC ul or honorable where subordination is wanting. Nor can any officer flatter himself of being crowned with military glory, while connected with an army defective in the essential of discipline, however meritorious his conduct may b
1 CONC e as an individual." A commanding officer of a regiment as head of a corps, has an undoubted right to order any of the soldiers upon any duty he thinks proper, and if he employs them in any improper service, he is accountable to h
1 CONC is superior only, to whom those who may think themselves aggrieved should apply for redress." 1 Orderly Book, August 20. Greene MSS.
1 CONT
1 CONT P234 Lee promptly replied : - " In consequence of your letter this forenoon I had put my troops in readiness to move. Your letter of this evening informed me that you had postponed the march of the army to the next day. Ishall nev
1 CONC ertheless move my infantry to-morrow eight miles on the road to Cross Creek, and follow the ensuing day with the cavalry. " Captain Oldham had better march the direct road from Ramsey's Mill to Cross Creek, and join my infantry at
1 CONC  Mclntosh's."
1 CONT
1 CONT P279 There he could take Marion for the first time by the hand, and tell him how highly he esteemed him. There he could converse confidentially with Lee, with whom he always rejoiced to take counsel; and there too he could thank, 
1 CONC by word of mouth, Finley of the artillery, and Oldham and Smith of the infantry, and young Eaton, who was soon to lay down his life for his country. It was a moment full of hopes, and of hopes that were not all to prove vain. On t
1 CONC he 14th, the post at Neilson's Ferry was evacuated. On the 13th, Lee, whose detachment was now called the van of the army, began his march for Fort Granby, at Friday's Ferry, about thirty miles west of Fort Motte, with minute inst
1 CONC ructions from Greene for acting upon the avidity of the commander, Major Maxwell, in order to force him to a prompt surrender. Pressing his march, Lee reached the fort in time to erect a battery in the night of the 14th.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1 Greene to Washington and Pres- of the Revolution, vol. iii. 
1 CONT p. 310. ident of Congress. Correspondence Lee's Memoirs, ch. xxix.
