1 CONT 8 September 1781 Battle of Eutaw Springs:  M. Lee Minnis, The First Virginia Regiment of Foot 1775-1783,  (Willow Bend Books, 1998), 103-104, At the Battle of Eutaw Springs, "the Americans lost 18 officers and 102 men killed, 43 o
1 CONC fficers and 332 men wounded, and eight missing.  This was about one-fourth of their strength but the Bristish lost almost half as 430 were captured in the battle and 72 more wounded taken as Stewart left them on the retreat.  This
1 CONC  last major battle for Greene in the south ranked as one of the most hotly contested encounters of the war... Capt. Robert Jouett of the 1st Continental Artillery relieved Capt. Oldham according to Pvt. Samuel Harris [See also Min
1 CONC nis at 276]. The Battlefield of Eutaw Springs now lies under Lake Marion.  The small village of that name lies east of Eutawville in Orangeburg County on S.C. 6 and 45."  Minnis, 251:  "The Battle of Eutaw Springs followed with LC
1 CONC ol. Campbell and Capt. Oldham killed; Capt. Thomas Edmunds took command of the regiment."
1 CONT
1 CONT 8 September 1781 DEATH:  Bryce Metcalf, Original Members and Other Officers Eligible to the Society of the Cincinnati 1783-1938,  (Strasburg, Virginia: Shenandoah Publishing House, Inc., 1938), 239, "Oldham, Conway, Captain.  Kill
1 CONC ed September 8, 1781.  Va. 8th Regiment Virginia Continental Line."  
1 CONT      Francis B. Heitman, Historical Register of Officers of the Continental Army During the War of the Revolution, (Washington, D.C.: The Rare Book Shop Pub. Co., 1914), 419.  "Oldham, Conway (Va). 2d Lieutenant 12th Virginia, 9th
1 CONC  November, 1776; 1st Lieutenant, 2d April, 1777; regiment [p.419] designated 8th Virginia, 14th September, 1778; Captain, —, 1780; killed at Eutaw Springs, 8th September, 1781." 
1 CONT      M. Lee Minnis, The First Virginia Regiment of Foot 1775-1783,  (Willow Bend Books, 1998), 321, "OLDHAM, Conway,  Captain.  Oldham was in the Eighth Virginia as of 14 September 1778.  Pvt. Samuel Harris reported that he joined
1 CONC  Capt. Oldham's Company on 7 October 1780.  The company became part of a regiment of militia commanded by Maj. Ridley and fought in the Battle of Guilford Courthouse.  Maj. Ridley was ousted for disobedience of orders in that batt
1 CONC le and the regiment was absorbed into LCol. Richard Cambell's First Virginia.  The battle of Hobkirk Hill (2nd Camden) and Ninety-Six followed in that regiment.  Capt. Oldham was killed at Eutaw Springs.  His land Warrant went to 
1 CONC Samuel Oldham, heir-at-law."    Minnis cites John H. Gwathmey, cp., Historical Register of Virginians in the Revolution (Richmond: The Dietz Press, 1938; reprint ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1987), 594;  Sele
1 CONC cted Records From Revolutionary War Pension and Bounty Land Warrant Application Files, 1800-1900, Series M805, Roll 402, S10813; and William Armstrong Crozier, ed., Virginia County Records, Vol. VI (Hasbrouck Heights, New Jersey: 
1 CONC  The Genealogical Association, 1909; reprint ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1993), 35.  Minnis), 429:  "Appendix:  Patriots Who Died in Service... Oldham, Conway  Capt.  8 Sep 81."
1 CONT      Letter from "R.C. Ballard Thruston, a member of the Society of Colonial Wars of Louisville, Ky., and a gentleman of antiquarian tastes," dated January 22, 1911 and reprinted in Kirk Q. Bigham, Major Abraham Kirkpatrick and Hi
1 CONC s Descendants, p16. Library of Congress, Washington, DC.  "The third son was Conway Oldham, killed at the battle of Eutaw Springs, S. C., September 8, 1781, then a captain in the Revolution and unmarried.  The land warrant for his
1 CONC  services being made out to his eldest brother and heir-at-law, Samuel Oldham." [Bounty land claim 503, Microfilm Series M-804, Roll 1840, National Archives, Washington, DC].  Bigham, 22:  "Conway Oldham, Va. 2nd Lieut., 12th Va. 
1 CONC Dec., 1776; 1st Lieut., April 2, 1777; regiment designated 8th Va., September 14, 1778; Capt., 1780; killed at Eutaw Springs, September 8, 1781 (See Heitman's Register, 312)."
1 CONT      Edward McCrady, LL.D., The History of South Carolina in the Revolution 1780-1783,  (New York: The MacMillan Company, 1902), 460, Footnote 2:  "Virginia Brigade, Lieut. Col. Campbell, Capt. Oldham, Lieut. Wilson killed; Captai
1 CONC ns Edmonds and Morgan, Lieutenants Miller and Jowitt wounded."
1 CONT      Patrick G. Wardell, Virginia/West Virginia Genealogical Data From Revolutionary War Pension and Bounty Land Warrant Records, Vol. 4,  (Heritage Books, Inc.), 37, "OLDHAM, Conway, esf [entered service from] 1777; KIA [killled 
1 CONC in action] 9/1781 Battle of Eutaw Springs, when captain; VA BLW [bounty land warrant] 14 issued 11/11/1782 to heir-at-law Samuel OLDHAM who gave power/o attorney 1807 Jefferson Co, VA, to agent to afb [apply for bounty land warran
1 CONC t]; power of attorney witnessed by James GALLOWAY Jr and William BARLOW, per that Co JP's George WILSON & John C MATHENY; Co clerk of court there then Worden POPE; BLW503 issued 4/12/1810 to Samuel OLDHAM. F-BLW503 [microfilm reel
1 CONC ] 1840."
1 CONT      E. M. Sanchez-Saavedra, A Guide to Virginia Military Organizations in the American Revolution, 1774-1787,  (Richmond, Virginia: Virginia State Library, 1978), 58, "Officers [8th Virginia Regiment of Foot], February 1781... Ca
1 CONC ptain Conway Oldham... Seven months after being arranged into the 8th Virginia Regiment, Captain Conway Oldham was killed in battle at Eutaw Springs.  The handful of uncaptured veterans served in various capacities until mustered 
1 CONC out in 1783."
1 CONT      Louis A. Burgess, ed., Virginia Soldiers of 1776,  (Richmond, Virginia: Richmond Press, Inc., 1929), 1020, "Captain Conway Oldham.  War Office, Richmond, Va. Nov. 1st, 1782.  'I do certify that the heirs of Capt. Conway Oldha
1 CONC m who was killed in the Battle of the Eutaw, in Sept., 1781, are entitled to such proportion of land as is allowed an officer in the line of this state, on the Contl. Establishment, from Apl., 1777 to the time of his death.  Agree
1 CONC able to a certificate in this office.' William Davis.  Warrant for 4000 acres issued to Susanna Oldham, her at law of Captain Conway Oldham, dec'd. the 11th of Nov., 1782.  Recorded Book I., page 3."
1 CONT
1 CONT      Gaius Marcus Brumbaugh, Revolutionary War Records, Volume 1, Virginia, (Lancaster Press, Lancaster, Pennsylvania: 1936), 108:  Revolutionary War Records VIRGINIA Section II (4), [Document No. 30--List No. I] "(4) List of Offi
1 CONC cers of the Army and Navy, Who Have Received Lands from Virginia for Revolutionary Services; the Quantity Received, the time of Service for Which Each Officer Received Land, &c. Down to September, 1833...[Name] Oldham, Conway [Ran
1 CONC k] Captain  [Line] Cont'l. [No. Acres] 4000 [When warrant obtained]  Nov. 11, 1782  [Time of service, etc.]  Apr. 1777 to date."
1 CONT      Gaius Marcus Brumbaugh, Revolutionary War Records, Volume 1, Virginia, (Lancaster Press, Lancaster, Pennsylvania: 1936), 387, 483: "(21) Virginia Military Land Warrants.  Virginia Military District of Ohio, Granted for Revolu
1 CONC tionary War Services, State Continental Line, Beginning August 8, 1782... [Number] 14 [Warrantees (A-B)] Oldham, Saml. (heir at law of Capt. Conway Oldham, decd.) [Rank and Service (C)] Capt. 3 years."
1 CONT
