0 @N6000000002906180125@ NOTE 1845 - executor of his father Daniel Rector's estate, Delaware County, Indiana.
0 @N6000000002906180086@ NOTE SOURCE:  Larry King, Rector Records, 1986. p14, 5-36.  Library of Congress No. CS71.R3 1986.  This family ["Phebe" Rector & James Sanders] lived in Delaware County, Indiana, Miami Co., OH, Jay Co., Indiana, and finally Page Co., I
1 CONC A. See also children 6-60, 6-61, 6-62, 6-491."
1 CONT
1 CONT RELIGION:  Methodist.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1860 CENSUS:   Perry Twp., Delaware Co., Indiana 819/819 In Household:
1 CONT James Sanders, age 40, born in VA, farmer
1 CONT Phebe Sanders, age 38, born in OH
1 CONT William K. Sanders, age 18, born in IN
1 CONT Nancy Sanders, age 17, born in IN
1 CONT Daniel Sanders, age 16, born in IN
1 CONT Aaron Sanders, age 15, born in IN
1 CONT Elizabeth Sanders, age 13, born in IN
1 CONT Mary Sanders, age 10, born in IN
1 CONT Eliza Jane Sanders, age 8, born in IN
1 CONT John W. Sanders, age 6, born in IN
1 CONT Alvin Sanders, age 3, born in IN
1 CONT Jeremiah Sanders, age 6/12, born in IN
1 CONT Nancy Rector, age 84, born in PA*
1 CONT CENSUS: *This is Phebe Sanders' mother, Nancy Garver Sanders; grandmother to Arthur Rector, who married Mary Cary, daughter of William Cary and Hannah Jackson.  Courtesy of Pam Miller, sewmap@cableone.net, http://awt.ancestry.com/
1 CONC cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=PED&db=sinatra&id=I276&style=TEXT.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1870 CENSUS: History of the Town of Hawleyville and Nebraska Township, Page County, Iowa,  (Before 1973), 23, "Sanders, James, 52, Farmer; Phoeba, 50, wife; Mary, 20, dau.; John, 17, son, Farm-Hand; Alvin, 12, son; Jerry, 9, son; 
1 CONC Clare, 8, son."
1 CONT
1 CONT 1880 CENSUS:  History of the Town of Hawleyville and Nebraska Township, Page County, Iowa,  (Before 1973), 71, "Sanders, James, 61, Farmer; Phoeba, 59, wife; Dennis, 22, son; Jerry, 20, son; Crow, Hattie, 18, Grand-daughter."  Not
1 CONC e that Alvin in 1870 census is identified as Dennis in the 1880 census.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1890 SOURCE: Biographical History of Page County, Iowa,  (Chicago: Lewis & Dunbar, Publishers, 1890), 745-746: JAMES SAUNDERS [sic, "Sanders"], one of the representative farmers of Nebraska Township, was born in Rockcastle County,
1 CONC  Kentucky, August 8, 1818, and is the son of Abednego and Amy (Cook) Saunders, natives of North Carolina.  There were thirteen children in the family, named as follows: Aaron, Wyatt, John M., deceased; Abednego, deceased; Jane, de
1 CONC ceased; James, Matilda, Caroline, Mary, Orinda, William, Elizabeth and Margaret. 
1 CONT
1 CONT      When James was nine years old his parents removed to Wayne County, Indiana, where they rented a farm for one year, and then removed to Delaware County, Indiana, locating on a farm. There the father died, May 7, 1835. James re
1 CONC mained at home, assisting with the farm work until he was [p746] twenty-two years of age.  He was married January 23, 1840, to Miss Phoebe Rector, a daughter of Daniel and Nancy (Garber) Rector. She was born September 22, 1820, in
1 CONC  Clarke County, Ohio. 
1 CONT
1 CONT      Mr. and Mrs. Saunders are the parents of thirteen children: Sarah Jane died when two years old, James S. died when three years old; Aaaron died in the army at the age of nineteen years; William, Nancy, wife of J. W. Cubbison;
1 CONC  Daniel, Elizabeth, wife of John W. Wheat; Emmaline Mary, wife of Charles Cole, Eliza Jane, wife of Abel Crow; J. W., Dennis, J. M., and Clara M., wife of Milton Cooper. 
1 CONT
1 CONT      After his marriage, Mr. Saunders remained for a time in Delaware County, and then went to Ohio, locating on a rented farm in Miami County; there he resided for seven years, when he returned to Delaware County, Indiana, and pu
1 CONC rchased eighty acres of wild land, which he improved and cultivated for six years. He disposed of this and bought a tract of eighty acres in the same county; for seven years he made this his home, and then sold out and moved to Ja
1 CONC y County, Indiana; he bought eighty acres and soon added another eighty acres, making this his home for eight years.  In 1869 he disposed of all his real estate and emigrated to Iowa. It was in October of this year that his mother
1 CONC  died, having made her home with his family for a number of years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT      Upon reaching Iowa he located in Hawleyville, Page County, where he remained a short time before renting a farm for the summer season; in the fall he purchased his present farm of eighty acres, which was slightly improved. He
1 CONC  has made all the improvements necessary to a well ordered farm, has a comfortable house, an orchard of an acre and a half, bearing fruit, and ten acres timber.  
1 CONT
1 CONT      Politically he is identified with the Republican party; he is the present Justice of the Peace, filling the office to the general satisfaction of the public. 
1 CONT
1 CONT      Mr. and Mrs. Saunders are members of the township Methodist Episcopal Church, in which they take a deep interest, and to which they contribute very liberally of their means."
1 CONT
1 CONT DEATH:  1922 History of Jay County, Indiana, by Dr. Jay, p126: "On September 12, 1868, John M. Wheat was united in marriage to Elizabeth Sanders, who was born in the neighboring county of Delaware, but was reared in Jay county, a 
1 CONC daughter of James and Phoebe Sanders, the former of whom was the owner of an eighty acre farm in Wayne township.  James Sanders and his wife later moved to Page county, Iowa, where their last days were spent."
1 CONT
1 CONT BURIAL:  History of the Town of Hawleyville and Nebraska Township, Page County, Iowa, Before 1973, 95, "SANDERS, James.  8 August 1818 - 23 April 1905.  Row 12 East Section.  SANDERS, Phoebe. 23 September 1820 - 9 June 1897.  Row 
1 CONC 12 East Section."  Photograph supplied by Dorothy Edwards, 10630 Ventris Ave., Garfield, AR 72732 on 25 March 1998 shows that the gravestones read "SANDERS  James Sanders Aug. 8, 1818  Apr. 23, 1905  Pheobe [sic] Sanders  Sept 23,
1 CONC  1820  June 9, 1897," and that to the left of the stone is a small marker that reads "Mother."
1 CONT
1 CONT Descendant Jerry Cain, <jerry@cainscape.com>, of Tucson, AZ is building a website at www.cainfamily.us, which now includes photos of James and Phoebe Sanders.
0 @N6000000002906180156@ NOTE !SOURCE:  Larry King, Rector Records, 1986. p14, 5-37.  Library of Congress
1 CONT No. CS71.R3 1986.
0 @N6000000002799118055@ NOTE "Mr. Moncure graduated A. B., William and Mary College, 1822. He was of the mercantile firm Dunlop, Moncure and Co., of Richmond, Va.; a communicant of Monumental Church, and lay delegate to the Diocesan Convention of Virginia, 18
1 CONC 39. He was also a life member of Virginia Historical Society. (History Monumental Church by Fisher; Moncure Family, Volume II, Chapter XIII.) " 
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes from James Madison's Montpelier NATIONAL TRUST FOR HISTORIC PRESERVATION
1 CONT HENRY W. MONCURE (1844 to 1848) 4 years 
1 CONT Henry Wood Moncure (1800-1866) was a merchant from Richmond. Initially he was deeded 750 acres (of Montpelier) under the condition that the title to the property would remain unencumbered. The agreement also contained the conditio
1 CONC n that Moncure could rent a portion of the house for three years until final payments had been made. It should be noted that he purchased not only the land and the house, but also many of the slaves and much of the personal proper
1 CONC ty, including furniture. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Evidently Payne Todd caused so much trouble that, after numerous attempts to sabotage the sale, Dolley sold it outright to Moncure in August 1844. In a touching note to Mr. Moncure, Dolley asks to come to Montpelier saying she wan
1 CONC ted ‘to return for a short time in order to assist in the arrangements of the household contents which must be in confusion - some of which I wish to retain.” She continued, “No one I think can appreciate my feeling of grief and d
1 CONC ismay at the necessity of transferring to another a beloved home.”
